{{Christopher Luxon sidebar}}
'''Christopher Mark Luxon''' (born 19 July 1970) is a New Zealand politician and businessman who has served as the 42nd [[prime minister of New Zealand]] since 2023. A member of the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]], he has been the member of Parliament (MP) for [[Botany (New Zealand electorate)|Botany]] since 2020 and previously served as [[Leader of the Opposition (New Zealand)|leader of the Opposition]] from 2021 to 2023. Prior to entering politics, he was the chief executive officer (CEO) of [[Air New Zealand]] from 2013 to 2019.

Luxon grew up in [[Howick, New Zealand|Howick]] in East Auckland before studying commerce at the [[University of Canterbury]]. He joined [[Unilever]] in 1993 and held senior roles at Unilever Canada, becoming president and CEO of the subsidiary in 2008. In 2011, he left Unilever Canada and joined Air New Zealand as group general manager and became CEO in 2013. After stepping down as CEO of Air New Zealand in 2019, Luxon won the pre-selection for the safe National Party seat of Botany in East Auckland. He won the [[leader of the New Zealand National Party|National Party leadership]] unopposed on [[2021 New Zealand National Party leadership election|30 November 2021]], eight months after giving his maiden speech, after the [[2021 New Zealand National Party leadership election|ousting]] of his predecessor. Luxon led his party to a plurality of seats at the [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023 general election]]. Luxon signed a coalition deal with [[ACT New Zealand]] and [[New Zealand First]] to form a majority, and was sworn in as prime minister on 27 November 2023.

Under Luxon's premiership, the [[Sixth National Government of New Zealand]] has shrunk New Zealand's [[civil service]] and attempted to boost the economy through international tourism. The government introduced cuts to healthcare expenditure, [[Sentencing (Reinstating Three Strikes) Amendment Act 2024|reintroduced]] a [[Three-strikes law|three-strikes sentencing law]] and a pilot military-style boot camp for youth offenders, and enacted NZ$14.7 billion in tax cuts. The government replaced the [[Three Waters reform programme|Three Waters]] reform programme with the [[Local Water Done Well]] policy, and minimised [[co-governance]] initiatives while discouraging the use of the [[Māori language]] alongside the English language in the public service. His government agreed to support the controversial [[Treaty Principles Bill]] to its first reading. In foreign policy, his government has increased military spending and [[Five Eyes]] cooperation in a general pro-United States shift. Luxon's coalition dynamics with his deputy prime ministers [[Winston Peters]] and [[David Seymour]] have proved controversial, given they have openly challenged his authority.<ref name="Luxon-2" /><ref name="P Poll" />

Luxon has struggled in [[Opinion polling for the 2026 New Zealand general election|opinion polls]] and remained largely unpopular with the New Zealand public. Rumours of potential challenges to his leadership in November 2025 and throughout 2026 led him to call a confidence vote in himself in April 2026, which he won. In [[August 2026 New Zealand National Party leadership election|August 2026]], he was unsuccessfully challenged for the leadership of the National Party by cabinet minister [[Chris Penk]] after he called another confidence vote in himself.

==Early life==
Christopher Mark Luxon<ref>{{Cite news |title=Who is the new National Party leader Christopher Luxon? |language=en |work=[[Newshub]] |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2021/11/who-is-the-new-national-party-leader-christopher-luxon.html |access-date=30 November 2021 |archive-date=30 November 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211130092142/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2021/11/who-is-the-new-national-party-leader-christopher-luxon.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> was born in Christchurch on 19 July 1970,<ref name="Scoop-2012"/> to a [[Roman Catholic]] family of Irish, Scottish and English descent.<ref name="Luxon">Luxon, Christopher; Mallard, Trevor: ''Maiden Statements CHRISTOPHER LUXON (National—Botany)'', New Zealand Parliament Hansard, Wellington, 24 March 2021</ref> His father, Graham Luxon, worked for [[Johnson & Johnson]] as a sales executive. His mother, Kathleen Luxon (née Turnbull), was a receptionist but subsequently enrolled in a Diploma of Social Work the same year that Chris started at university. She eventually worked as a psychotherapist and counsellor.<ref>[https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/456934/who-is-the-new-national-party-leader-christopher-luxon Who is the new National Party leader Christopher Luxon?] RNZ, 1 December 2021.</ref><ref name="Luxon" />

The family lived in Christchurch until Christopher was seven, when they moved to [[Howick, New Zealand|Howick]] in [[Auckland]]. After a year at [[Saint Kentigern College]] and another year at [[Howick College]], the family returned to Christchurch and Luxon spent three years at [[Christchurch Boys' High School]].<ref name="Bradley-2013">{{cite news |last1=Bradley |first1=Grant |date=7 June 2013 |title=Air NZ's flying salesman |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/news/article.cfm?c_id=3&objectid=10888926 |access-date=12 November 2019 |archive-date=6 November 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191106063807/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/news/article.cfm?c_id=3&objectid=10888926 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Espiner |first1=Guyon |date=24 February 2016 |title=Christopher Luxon: New heights of success |work=[[New Zealand Listener]] |url=https://www.noted.co.nz/currently/currently-profiles/christopher-luxon-new-heights-of-success |url-status=live |access-date=10 November 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200821212137/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/117151149/national-chooses-christopher-luxon-as-botany-mp |archive-date=21 August 2020}}</ref> While there, he won the prize for senior debating.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Manhire |first=Toby |date=18 September 2023 |title=Let the debates begin |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/18-09-2023/let-the-debates-begin |access-date=18 September 2023 |website=[[The Spinoff]] |archive-date=19 September 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230919090905/https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/18-09-2023/let-the-debates-begin |url-status=live }}</ref> He studied at the [[University of Canterbury]] from 1989 to 1992, gaining a Master of Commerce (Business Administration) degree.<ref name="Stuff-2021">{{Cite web|date=30 November 2021|title=Former Air NZ CEO Christopher Luxon showed 'enormous' intellectual capability but rarely met with 'ordinary workers'|url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/business/industries/127097008/former-air-nz-ceo-christopher-luxon-showed-enormous-intellectual-capability-but-rarely-met-with-ordinary-workers|access-date=30 November 2021|website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |language=en|archive-date=30 November 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211130030456/https://www.stuff.co.nz/business/industries/127097008/former-air-nz-ceo-christopher-luxon-showed-enormous-intellectual-capability-but-rarely-met-with-ordinary-workers|url-status=live}}</ref>

He always wanted to be a businessman and said: "If you met me at 12 years old I'd be having window washing rounds, lawn mowing rounds and deck painting. I just loved it."<ref>{{Cite web |date=7 June 2022 |title=How Christopher Luxon is rebranding the National Party |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/468615/how-christopher-luxon-is-rebranding-the-national-party |access-date=7 August 2026 |website=RNZ |language=en-nz}}</ref> During his high school and university years, Luxon worked [[Part-time job|part-time]] at [[McDonald's New Zealand|McDonald's]] and as a [[Bellhop|porter]] at the [[Crowne Plaza, Christchurch|Parkroyal Hotel]].<ref name="Radio New Zealand-2022">{{Cite news |date=7 June 2022 |title=How Christopher Luxon is rebranding the National Party |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/in-depth/468615/how-christopher-luxon-is-rebranding-the-national-party |access-date=26 September 2023 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |language=en-nz |archive-date=26 September 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230926054751/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/in-depth/468615/how-christopher-luxon-is-rebranding-the-national-party |url-status=live}}</ref>

==Business career==
=== Unilever ===
Luxon worked for [[Unilever]] from 1993 to 2011, starting in [[Wellington]] as a management trainee for two years, leaving for [[Sydney]] in 1995. He worked his way up in the company, working in Sydney until 2000, in London from 2000 to 2003, and then Chicago from 2003 to 2008, becoming "Global Deodorants and Grooming Category" Director.<ref>{{Cite news |title=John Campbell: What exactly has the tide brought in? |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2023/10/28/john-campbell-what-exactly-has-the-tide-brought-in/ |access-date=17 June 2024 |work=[[1News]] |language=en |archive-date=17 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240617141755/https://www.1news.co.nz/2023/10/28/john-campbell-what-exactly-has-the-tide-brought-in/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In 2008 when Unilever restructured, he became president of the company's Canadian operations, based in [[Toronto]].<ref name="Radio New Zealand-2022" /><ref>{{Cite news |last=McCready |first=Tim |date=23 November 2023 |title=Mood of the Boardroom: Luxon breathes new life into the party |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/mood-of-the-boardroom-christopher-luxon-breathes-new-life-into-the-party/QLMTQSDN7BJDCTH55WVRBZ5GUE/ |access-date=22 November 2023 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |language=en-NZ |archive-date=22 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231122234513/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/mood-of-the-boardroom-christopher-luxon-breathes-new-life-into-the-party/QLMTQSDN7BJDCTH55WVRBZ5GUE/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Altogether, he worked overseas for 16 years before returning to New Zealand.<ref>{{Cite web |date=14 July 2024 |title=Luxon's corporate career leaves him well prepped for political pomp and ceremony |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/522088/luxon-s-corporate-career-leaves-him-well-prepped-for-political-pomp-and-ceremony |access-date=7 August 2026 |website=RNZ |language=en-nz}}</ref>

=== Air New Zealand ===
Luxon joined [[Air New Zealand]] as group general manager in May 2011 and was named the chief executive officer on 19 June 2012, taking up the role at the end of that year.<ref name="Scoop-2012">{{cite web|url=http://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/BU1206/S00572/air-new-zealand-announces-new-chief-executive-officer.htm |title=Air New Zealand Announces New Chief Executive Officer |publisher=[[Scoop (website)|Scoop]] |date=19 June 2012 |access-date=30 June 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190611033804/http://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/BU1206/S00572/air-new-zealand-announces-new-chief-executive-officer.htm|archive-date=11 June 2019|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Godfery |first=Morgan |date=3 December 2021 |title=Christopher Luxon is out of step with most New Zealanders – can he really challenge Ardern? |language=en-GB |work=[[The Guardian]] |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/commentisfree/2021/dec/04/christopher-luxon-is-out-of-step-with-most-new-zealanders-can-he-really-challenge-ardern |access-date=4 July 2023 |issn=0261-3077 |archive-date=4 July 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230704020715/https://www.theguardian.com/world/commentisfree/2021/dec/04/christopher-luxon-is-out-of-step-with-most-new-zealanders-can-he-really-challenge-ardern |url-status=live }}</ref>  During his eight-year leadership, Air New Zealand profits grew to record levels. Important contributors to this were a booming tourism market; the company's decision to stop operating under-performing regional routes; and the cutting of hundreds of jobs.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Walters |first=Laura |date=21 May 2024 |title=Christopher Luxon the disciplinarian |url=https://newsroom.co.nz/2024/05/22/christopher-luxon-the-disciplinarian/ |access-date=7 August 2026 |website=Newsroom |language=en-US}}</ref> The company was named Australia's most trusted brand several times.<ref name="Stuff-2021"/>

In 2014, Luxon joined the board of [[Virgin Australia]] representing Air NZ, which was then a major shareholder.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Anthony |first=John |date=6 July 2014 |title=Air NZ chief takes seat with Virgin board |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/business/industries/10240205/Air-NZ-chief-takes-seat-with-Virgin-board |access-date=18 November 2023 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |language=en |archive-date=18 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231118003539/https://www.stuff.co.nz/business/industries/10240205/Air-NZ-chief-takes-seat-with-Virgin-board |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |title=Air NZ boosts Virgin holding to the max |url=https://www.smh.com.au/business/air-nz-boosts-virgin-holding-to-the-max-20110926-1ktig.html |access-date=18 November 2023 |work=[[The Sydney Morning Herald]] |date=26 September 2011 |archive-date=18 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231118003549/https://amp.smh.com.au/business/air-nz-boosts-virgin-holding-to-the-max-20110926-1ktig.html |url-status=live }}</ref> In 2016, Air NZ decided it was no longer in its best interest to maintain a close connection with another airline so it sold its 25.9% stake in Virgin Australia. Luxon consequently left the Virgin board.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mandow |first=Nikki |date=12 April 2018 |title=Inside Air New Zealand's divorce from Virgin |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/business/opinion-analysis/103041222/inside-air-new-zealands-divorce-from-virgin |access-date=7 August 2026 |website=Stuff |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|last=Hooton |first=Matthew |date=16 November 2023 |title=Whatever happened to those Christopher Luxon negotiating skills? |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/matthew-hooton-whatever-happened-to-those-christopher-luxon-negotiating-skills/BHVSDPU53RBTLD55A7R2PTE4CU/ |access-date=16 November 2023 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |archive-date=16 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231116171347/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/matthew-hooton-whatever-happened-to-those-christopher-luxon-negotiating-skills/BHVSDPU53RBTLD55A7R2PTE4CU/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|last=Bradley |first=Grant |date=31 March 2016 |title=Air NZ set to ditch Virgin stake |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/air-nz-set-to-ditch-virgin-stake/KV22CM5HPI6VZZVKEDSHYIIW44/?c_id=3&objectid=11614171 |access-date=16 November 2016 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |archive-date=26 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230626072657/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/air-nz-set-to-ditch-virgin-stake/KV22CM5HPI6VZZVKEDSHYIIW44/?c_id=3&objectid=11614171 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Luxon resigned from Air NZ in 2019, and hinted at a possible career with the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Air New Zealand chief executive Christopher Luxon resigns, hints at political move |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/news/article.cfm?c_id=3&objectid=12242056 |access-date=10 November 2019 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=20 June 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200407042510/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/news/article.cfm?c_id=3&objectid=12242056|archive-date=7 April 2020}}</ref> He was encouraged in this by his close friend, former Prime Minister [[John Key]], who said Luxon would be a "world class candidate" for the National Party.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Cooke |first=Henry |date=10 October 2019 |title=John Key endorses Christopher Luxon as 'world class' National candidate |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/116485669/exair-nz-boss-christopher-luxon-keen-to-stand-for-national-party-in-botany |access-date=7 August 2026 |website=Stuff |language=en}}</ref>

== Personal wealth ==
As at March 2024, his net worth was estimated to be between NZ$21 million and NZ$30 million, which makes him the second-wealthiest leader of the National Party, after former Prime Minister John Key. He then owned seven properties, including a home in Remuera valued at NZ$7.68 million, and other assets collectively valued at over NZ$21 million.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Chapman |first=Madeleine |date=1 March 2024 |title=The rich stay rich by pretending to be poor |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/01-03-2024/the-rich-stay-rich-by-pretending-to-be-poor |access-date=7 August 2026 |website=The Spinoff |language=en}}</ref> In late 2024, Luxon stated, "Let's be clear, I'm wealthy," in response to questions about his property sales and financial position.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Franks |first=Raphael |title='I get it, I'm wealthy': Luxon on media attention from $890k apartment sale |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/prime-minister-christopher-luxon-responds-to-attention-on-wellington-apartment-sale-on-newstalk-zb/GZNTL5LEBVFFPKG5GKOGIYMTP4/ |access-date=7 August 2026 |website=NZ Herald |language=en-NZ}}</ref>

After becoming prime minister, Luxon stayed in his own Wellington apartment and claimed a NZ$52,000 accommodation allowance, to which he said he was entitled. After public scrutiny began, he quickly changed his position, saying: "It's clear that the issue of my accommodation allowance is becoming a distraction" and "As such, I have decided today that I will no longer claim the allowance and will repay anything I have received since I became Prime Minister."<ref>{{cite news |title=PM Christopher Luxon says he will pay back his accommodation allowance |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/510614/pm-christopher-luxon-says-he-will-pay-back-his-accommodation-allowance |access-date=15 October 2025 |publisher=RNZ |date=1 March 2024}}</ref>

Luxon sold three of his properties in 2024 with ''Stuff'' estimating he could have made up to $769,500 in profit.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Witton |first=Bridie |date=30 October 2025 |title=Hipkins' $600k house flipping attack on me is a diversion from Labour's failures, says Luxon |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360870001/luxon-dismisses-hipkins-capital-gains-attack-political-distraction |access-date=7 August 2026 |website=Stuff |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Taunton |first1=Esther |title=PM Christopher Luxon sells third property of 2024 |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/home-property/360504662/pm-christopher-luxon-sells-third-property-2024 |work=Stuff |date=3 December 2024}}</ref>

==Early political career==
=== Member of Parliament ===
{{See also|Electoral history of Christopher Luxon}}
In November 2019, Luxon secured the National Party candidacy for the [[Botany (New Zealand electorate)|Botany]] electorate – which has always been won by National and was regarded as a [[safe seat]] for them<ref>{{cite news |last1=Block |first1=George |date=4 November 2019 |title=National chooses former Air NZ boss Christopher Luxon as Botany MP candidate |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/117151149/national-chooses-christopher-luxon-as-botany-mp |url-status=live |access-date=10 November 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200821212137/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/117151149/national-chooses-christopher-luxon-as-botany-mp |archive-date=21 August 2020}}</ref> – after [[Jami-Lee Ross]] resigned from the party.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Jami-Lee Ross tweets pics of Simon Bridges and Chinese '$100k donor' as MP accuses National Party leader of electoral fraud |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2018/10/16/jami-lee-ross-tweets-pics-of-simon-bridges-and-chinese-100k-donor-as-mp-accuses-national-party-leader-of-electoral-fraud/ |access-date=29 June 2022 |work=[[1News]] |language=en |archive-date=29 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220629173620/https://www.1news.co.nz/2018/10/16/jami-lee-ross-tweets-pics-of-simon-bridges-and-chinese-100k-donor-as-mp-accuses-national-party-leader-of-electoral-fraud/ |url-status=live }}</ref> 
