===In Opposition (2020–2023)===
Amid a sweep of National seats lost to Labour in the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020 general election]], Luxon won Botany with 52% of the vote.<ref>{{cite web |title=Botany – Official Result |url=https://electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2020/electorate-details-04.html |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=7 November 2020 |archive-date=2 July 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230702234406/https://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2020/electorate-details-04.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Small |first1=Zane |title=NZ Election 2020 – Winners and losers: Chris Luxon a victory for National but Labour flips flood of seats red |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2020/10/nz-election-2020-winners-and-losers-chris-luxon-a-victory-for-national-but-labour-flips-flood-of-seats-red.html |access-date=18 October 2020 |work=[[Newshub]] |date=18 October 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201018102006/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2020/10/nz-election-2020-winners-and-losers-chris-luxon-a-victory-for-national-but-labour-flips-flood-of-seats-red.html |archive-date=18 October 2020|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Weekes |first1=John |title=Election 2020: Chris Luxon cruises to victory as new Botany MP |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300131308/election-2020-chris-luxon-cruises-to-victory-as-new-botany-mp |access-date=18 October 2020 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=18 October 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201018102513/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300131308/election-2020-chris-luxon-cruises-to-victory-as-new-botany-mp |archive-date=18 October 2020|url-status=live}}</ref> He was appointed as the spokesperson for local government, research, science, manufacturing and land information, as well as associate spokesperson for transport in the [[Shadow Cabinet of Judith Collins]].<ref>{{cite web |date=30 November 2021 |title=Live: Chris Luxon to lead the National Party |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300466178/live-chris-luxon-to-lead-the-national-party |access-date=30 November 2021 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |archive-date=30 November 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211130005858/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300466178/live-chris-luxon-to-lead-the-national-party |url-status=live }}</ref> After Collins was removed as party leader on 25 November, Luxon was cited as a potential replacement.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/127101873/national-leadership-the-top-contenders-to-replace-judith-collins |title=National leadership: The top contenders to replace Judith Collins |date=25 November 2021 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |access-date=26 November 2021 |archive-date=26 November 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211126035157/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/127101873/national-leadership-the-top-contenders-to-replace-judith-collins |url-status=live }}</ref> On 30 November, after Bridges's withdrew from the [[2021 New Zealand National Party leadership election|National Party leadership election]], Luxon was announced as the party's leader.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/456886/christopher-luxon-set-to-be-new-national-party-leader-as-simon-bridges-withdraws |title=Christopher Luxon voted new National Party leader as Simon Bridges withdraws |date=30 October 2021 |access-date=30 October 2021 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211130073501/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/456886/christopher-luxon-voted-new-national-party-leader-as-simon-bridges-withdraws|archive-date=30 November 2021|url-status=live}}</ref>

As Leader of the Opposition, Luxon used public funding to pay for [[Māori language]] (''te reo'') lessons. He defended using taxpayer money to pay for his Māori language lessons, stating that developing better skills in Māori was highly relevant to his role as Opposition leader and a potential Prime Minister.<ref name="Radio New Zealand-2023">{{cite news |title=Christopher Luxon's reo Māori lessons paid for by taxpayer |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/504994/christopher-luxon-s-reo-maori-lessons-paid-for-by-taxpayer |access-date=21 December 2023 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=18 December 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231218035225/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/504994/christopher-luxon-s-reo-maori-lessons-paid-for-by-taxpayer|archive-date=18 December 2023|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Coughlan |first1=Thomas |title=PM Christopher Luxon defends taxpayer funding for his te reo Māori lessons after hypocrisy claims |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/chirstopher-luxon-defends-spending-on-te-reo-maori-lessons/62KV3CEMEJFVFKHODZRDK7KAME/ |access-date=21 December 2023 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=19 December 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231219205249/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/chirstopher-luxon-defends-spending-on-te-reo-maori-lessons/62KV3CEMEJFVFKHODZRDK7KAME/ |archive-date=19 December 2023}}</ref> Once he became Prime Minister, the [[Sixth National Government of New Zealand|National-led coalition government]] considered discontinuing incentive payments for public servants to learn the Māori language.<ref name="Radio New Zealand-2023" />

