{{Further information|Sixth National Government of New Zealand}}
In the [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023 general election]],<ref>{{cite news |last=Graham-McLay |first=Charlotte |title=From selling deodorant to running the country: New Zealand's new PM, Christopher Luxon |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/oct/14/from-selling-deodorant-to-running-the-country-new-zealands-new-pm-christopher-luxon |access-date=7 November 2023 |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=14 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231106192458/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/oct/14/from-selling-deodorant-to-running-the-country-new-zealands-new-pm-christopher-luxon |archive-date=6 November 2023|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Perry |first=Nick |title=New Zealand elects conservative Christopher Luxon as premier after 6 years of liberal rule |url=https://apnews.com/article/new-zealand-election-luxon-hipkins-ardern-9309fc1816569adac27efe0e7ec77c25 |access-date=20 October 2023 |work=[[Associated Press News]] |date=15 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231015150908/https://apnews.com/article/new-zealand-election-luxon-hipkins-ardern-9309fc1816569adac27efe0e7ec77c25 |archive-date=15 October 2023}}</ref> Luxon retained his [[Botany (New Zealand electorate)|Botany electorate]] seat with 67% of the vote.<ref>{{cite web |title=Botany – Official Result |url=https://archive.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2023/electorate-details-04.html |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=26 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231123104117/https://archive.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2023/electorate-details-04.html |archive-date=23 November 2023 |date=3 November 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref> The National Party won 38.1% of the party vote and 48 seats – the most of any party but not enough to govern outright.<ref>{{Cite web |date=3 November 2023|title=National and Act lose majority in final vote count |url=https://www.newsroom.co.nz/page/national-and-act-lose-majority-in-final-vote-count |access-date=7 November 2023 |website=[[Newsroom (website)|Newsroom]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231103110034/https://www.newsroom.co.nz/page/national-and-act-lose-majority-in-final-vote-count|archive-date=3 November 2023|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Formation of coalition government ===
National required support from at least two other parties to form a government. They negotiated with [[ACT New Zealand|ACT]] and  [[New Zealand First]] for six weeks to produce New Zealand's first formal three-party coalition government.<ref>Shaw, Richard (24 November 2023). [https://theconversation.com/three-parties-two-deals-one-government-the-stress-points-within-new-zealands-coalition-of-many-colours-217673#:~:text=Unsurprisingly%2C%20in%20this%20coalition%20of,the%20bar%20he%20has%20set. Three parties, two deals, one government: the stress points within New Zealand's 'coalition of many colours']. ''The Conversation''.</ref> The negotiations took until 24 November<ref>{{cite news |last1=Quinlivan |first1=Mark |title=Election 2023: National, ACT and NZ First's Coalition agreement |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2023/11/election-2023-national-act-and-nz-first-s-coalition-agreement.html |access-date=27 November 2023 |work=[[Newshub]] |date=24 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231124040207/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2023/11/election-2023-national-act-and-nz-first-s-coalition-agreement.html |archive-date=24 November 2023}}</ref> and involved two separate coalition agreements – one between National and ACT, and one between National and New Zealand First – with each junior party formally committing to support the other's key priorities.<ref>{{Cite web |date=25 March 2024 |title=CO (24) 2: National, ACT and New Zealand First Coalition Government: Consultation and Operating Arrangements {{!}} Department of the Prime Minister and Cabinet (DPMC) |url=https://www.dpmc.govt.nz/publications/co-24-2-national-act-and-new-zealand-first-coalition-government-consultation-and-operating-arrangements |access-date=7 August 2026 |website=www.dpmc.govt.nz |language=en}}</ref> Luxon was sworn in as prime minister by Governor-General [[Cindy Kiro|Dame Cindy Kiro]] on 27 November.<ref>{{cite news |title=Watch: Christopher Luxon, new ministers sworn in at Government House |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/503352/watch-christopher-luxon-new-ministers-sworn-in-at-government-house |access-date=27 November 2023 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=27 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231127054606/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/503352/watch-christopher-luxon-new-ministers-sworn-in-at-government-house |archive-date=27 November 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Christopher Luxon sworn in as New Zealand's new prime minister |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2023/11/27/christopher-luxon-sworn-in-as-new-zealands-new-prime-minister/ |access-date=27 November 2023 |agency=[[1News]]  |date=27 November 2023 |archive-date=27 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231127001802/https://www.1news.co.nz/2023/11/27/christopher-luxon-sworn-in-as-new-zealands-new-prime-minister/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Economic decline===

On 18 February 2024, Luxon delivered his first State of the Nation address, in which he blamed the previous [[Sixth Labour Government of New Zealand|Labour Government]] for what he called the "precarious state of the economy". Luxon said that economic growth is the central theme of his government's agenda, that it is the solution to New Zealand's problems and key to improving living standards for all New Zealanders. He said his Government would restore the economy by cutting taxes (by $14.7 billion),<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Matheson |first1=Anna |last2=Elliffe |first2=Craig |last3=Wesselbaum |first3=Dennis |last4=Thabrew |first4=Hiran |last5=Talbot-Jones |first5=Julia |last6=Barrow |first6=Mark |last7=Welch |first7=Timothy |date=30 May 2024 |title=NZ Budget 2024: 'tax relief' for the 'squeezed middle' – but who's paying? 7 experts follow the money |url=https://theconversation.com/nz-budget-2024-tax-relief-for-the-squeezed-middle-but-whos-paying-7-experts-follow-the-money-230102 |access-date=7 August 2026 |website=The Conversation |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=11 December 2023 |title=The government's tax cuts and changes explained |url=https://hnry.co.nz/resources/news/governments-tax-plan-explained/ |access-date=7 August 2026 |website=hnry.co.nz |language=en-nz}}</ref> reducing public spending and "[[red tape]]".<ref>{{cite news |title=Watch: 'State of the nation is fragile', Christopher Luxon says |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/509471/watch-state-of-the-nation-is-fragile-christopher-luxon-says |access-date=21 February 2024 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=19 February 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240220015428/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/509471/watch-state-of-the-nation-is-fragile-christopher-luxon-says |archive-date=20 February 2024}}</ref>

However, under his government, the economy entered a protracted recession, with per capita GDP falling 4.6% since September 2022 due to tight monetary policy combating inflation that peaked at 7.3%. Unemployment rose from 3.7% in June 2023 to 5.2% by late 2025.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Brunskill |first=Dan |date=22 December 2025 |title=Sorry! The economic recovery you ordered has been delayed |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/business/22-12-2025/sorry-the-economic-recovery-you-ordered-has-been-delayed |access-date=7 August 2026 |website=The Spinoff |language=en}}</ref>
  
As part of reducing Government spending, Luxon's government began implementing significant cutbacks to New Zealand's public service in late 2023. Radio New Zealand reported in May 2025 that the coalition had cut 9,500 public sector jobs.<ref>{{cite news |title=How many public sector jobs have really been axed? |first=Lauren |last=Crimp |date=28 May 2025 |url= https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/562350/how-many-public-sector-jobs-have-really-been-axed |publisher= RNZ |access-date=1 September 2026}}</ref> More than 240 government programmes have been scaled down or scrapped.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Public service cuts becoming clear as Nicola Willis forecasts 'frontline' Budget |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/budget-2024-government-public-service-cuts-becoming-clear-as-finance-minister-forecasts-frontline-budget/JE6YBE5LDFHATC3UZ7JSSJ7HY4/ |access-date=7 August 2026 |website=NZ Herald}}</ref>  Luxon has consistently articulated the rationale for these cuts, amounting to about $6 billion,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Trevett |first=Claire |title=Use our tax calculator to see what's in the Budget for you |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/budget-2024-tax-calculator-see-how-tax-cuts-affect-you-and-whats-in-it-for-you/CTMRUYLIL5GPTAMUZVYDB7VJPA/ |access-date=7 August 2026 |website=NZ Herald}}</ref> and publicly defended the scale of job losses.<ref>{{Cite web |title='More medical doctors, not spin doctors': PM defends public sector job cuts |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/prime-minister-christopher-luxon-defends-public-sector-job-cuts-govt-wants-more-medical-doctors-not-spin-doctors/UPDRZGG7OJAZFDCQUZ3K7ORWIQ/ |access-date=7 August 2026 |website=NZ Herald}}</ref>

=== Health sector ===

New Zealand's health system faced severe strains before the coalition government came to power, primarily from workforce shortages, financial mismanagement, and long wait times for many services. Core problems included a significant shortage in the number of GPs leading to a massive increase in visits to hospital emergency departments plus financial mismanagement. Health NZ posted a $722 million deficit in 2023.<ref>{{Cite web |date=7 March 2025 |title=Health New Zealand lost control of spending, Deloitte report finds |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/544103/health-new-zealand-lost-control-of-spending-deloitte-report-finds |access-date=7 August 2026 |website=RNZ |language=en-nz}}</ref>

In response, the government replaced the board of Health NZ with a commissioner to improve performance and accountability.<ref>{{Cite web |date=30 July 2024 |title=Health NZ changes: 'Everything's on the table' – Luxon |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/523527/health-nz-changes-everything-s-on-the-table-luxon |access-date=7 August 2026 |website=RNZ |language=en-nz}}</ref> This was followed by multiple high-profile resignations, including the Director-General of Health, the Chief Executive of Health NZ, and the Director of Public Health.<ref>[https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/542149/massive-body-count-in-health-due-to-targets-falling-short-labour-says 'Massive body count' in health due to targets falling short – Labour says], RNZ, 17 February 2025</ref> In January 2025, Luxon announced that [[Simeon Brown]] would replace [[Shane Reti]] as [[Minister of Health (New Zealand)|Minister of Health]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Day |first=Tom |date=19 January 2025 |title=Shane Reti out as Health Minister as PM makes major cabinet reshuffle |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2025/01/19/shane-reti-out-as-health-minister-as-pm-makes-major-cabinet-reshuffle/ |access-date=19 January 2025|work=[[1News]] |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250119113652/https://www.1news.co.nz/2025/01/19/shane-reti-out-as-health-minister-as-pm-makes-major-cabinet-reshuffle/|archive-date=19 January 2025|url-status=live}}</ref>

Under the coalition agreement with ACT and NZ First, Luxon controversially agreed to repeal New Zealand's [[Smokefree Environments and Regulated Products (Smoked Tobacco) Amendment Act 2022|smoke-free legislation]] that would have made it illegal for anyone born in 2008 or later to legally purchase tobacco. Researchers from the University of Otago warned that thousands of New Zealanders will die needlessly, and that the repeal "flies in the face of robust research evidence."<ref>{{Cite web |work=University of Otago |date=27 February 2024 |title=Repeal of smokefree laws means thousands will die, researchers warn |url=https://www.otago.ac.nz/news/newsroom/repeal-of-smokefree-laws-means-thousands-will-die,-researchers-warn |access-date=7 August 2026 |language=en}}</ref> Chris Hipkins suggested the government is "firmly in the pocket of the tobacco industry."<ref>{{cite news |title=Smokefree smoking law repeal: Enraged Labour leader Chris Hipkins gives impassioned speech |first=Adam |last=Pearse |date=28 February 2024 |url= https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/enraged-labour-leader-chris-hipkins-gives-impassioned-speech-amid-repeal-of-anti-smoking-legislation/4U3FFOJRF5DIXDSLOKXR3XBUHA/ |newspaper=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |access-date=1 September 2026}}</ref>

=== Law and order ===
Luxon's government promised on a "tough on crime" agenda based on tougher sentencing, expanded police powers and targeted measures on gangs and youth offending. His government: 
* brought back the controversial three strikes regime, increasing mandatory sentencing for specified serious repeat offenders;<ref>[https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/tougher-sentences-ahead-three-strikes-returns Tougher sentences ahead as Three Strikes returns], Beehive, 17 June 2025</ref>
* brought back the [[Boot camps in New Zealand|boot‑camp model]] for serious and persistent young offenders, despite multiple studies, official data, and expert commentary spanning several decades which indicate that boot camps do not reduce reoffending.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Opinion: Boot camps are back – the psychological evidence they don't work never went away |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/boot-camps-for-young-offenders-are-back-the-psychological-evidence-they-dont-work-never-went-away/R7LYXFRJSRDIFAUM2HRJTKEL5I/ |access-date=7 August 2026 |website=NZ Herald |language=en-NZ}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Sowman-Lund |first=Stewart |date=17 November 2022 |title=The cases for and against National's youth offender 'boot camps' |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/17-11-2022/the-cases-for-and-against-nationals-youth-offender-boot-camps |access-date=7 August 2026 |website=The Spinoff |language=en}}</ref> 80% of the participants in the pilot re-offended within 12-months which is the same rate of youth re-offending without intervention.<ref>{{Cite web |last=McConnell |first=Glenn |date=8 August 2025 |title=Bootcamp re-offending rate revealed: 80% allegedly offended within the year |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360784864/bootcamp-re-offending-rate-revealed-80-allegedly-offended-within-year |access-date=7 August 2026 |website=Stuff |language=en}}</ref>
* banned gang patches in public and made gang membership an aggravating factor in sentencing;<ref>[https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/gang-crackdown-begins-stroke-midnight Gang crackdown begins at stroke of midnight], Beehive release 20 November 2024</ref> 
* announced an extra $1.9 billion for Corrections to expand prison capacity and hire around 700 more Corrections officers;<ref name="times.co.nz">{{Cite web |last=Harrowell |first=Chris |date=28 August 2024 |title=Luxon vows policy changes will reduce crime |url=https://www.times.co.nz/news/luxon-vows-policy-changes-will-reduce-crime/ |access-date=7 August 2026 |website=Times |language=en-NZ}}</ref>
* committed to recruiting 500 additional frontline police officers by the end of 2025 (a target the Government has struggled to fully meet on time)<ref>{{Cite web |date=27 November 2025 |title=Minister defends 'ambitious' goal as coalition officially misses 500 new police target |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/580108/minister-defends-ambitious-goal-as-coalition-officially-misses-500-new-police-target |access-date=7 August 2026 |website=RNZ |language=en-nz}}</ref>
* stopped funding for section 27 [[cultural report]]s<ref name="times.co.nz"/> which Chief Justice [[Heemi Taumaunu]] said "provided "the court valuable information about the defendant."<ref>{{Cite web |title=Norris Ward McKinnon Annual Lecture 2020 {{!}} The District Court of New Zealand |url=https://www.districtcourts.govt.nz/reports-publications-and-statistics/publications/norris-ward-mckinnon-annual-lecture-2020 |access-date=7 August 2026 |website=www.districtcourts.govt.nz}}</ref>
* provided $78 million to extend rehabilitation programs to prisoners on remand.<ref>[https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/19-billion-investment-keep-nz-safe-crime $1.9 billion investment to keep NZ safe from crime], Beehive release 6 May 2024</ref>

As the prison population approached 11,000 in November 2025, Luxon publicly endorsed policies which lead to higher prisoner numbers as "a good thing" as it keeps offenders off the streets.

Luxon also defended the boot camp programme, saying the government was "going to try something different because we cannot carry on getting the results that we've been getting."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hu |first1=Justin |title='I don't care': Fired-up Luxon defends boot camps for youth offenders |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/06/25/i-dont-care-fired-up-luxon-defends-boot-camps-for-youth-offenders/ |access-date=12 January 2025 |work=[[1News]] |date=24 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241129102638/https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/06/25/i-dont-care-fired-up-luxon-defends-boot-camps-for-youth-offenders/ |archive-date=29 November 2024|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Treaty Principles Bill ===
As part of the coalition agreement with [[ACT New Zealand]], Luxon agreed to support the [[Treaty Principles Bill]] at its first reading and send it to a select committee. Luxon made it clear that National would not support the bill beyond that stage or allow it to become law. He has emphasised that the bill does a disservice to the Treaty of Waitangi by oversimplifying 184 years of complex debate and discussion around the Treaty.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ensor |first=Jamie |title='Very simplistic': Luxon pushes back against Seymour ahead of Treaty Principles Bill vote |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/treaty-principles-bill-to-finally-face-first-vote-as-opposition-looms-large-both-inside-outside-parliament/XFIWI3S3NFDDVOIW3FPXPH6KU4/ |access-date=7 August 2026 |website=NZ Herald |language=en-NZ}}</ref> The bill sparked heated debate in Parliament with MPs describing it as "divisive" and a "wrecking ball" to Crown-Māori relations.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Treaty Principles Bill passes first reading amid haka interruption |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/11/14/treaty-principles-bill-passes-first-reading-amid-haka-interruption/ |access-date=7 August 2026 |website=1News |language=en}}</ref>

=== Environmental issues ===

In early December 2023, Luxon along with Cyclone Recovery Minister [[Mark Mitchell (New Zealand politician)|Mark Mitchell]] and Transport and Local Government Minister [[Simeon Brown]], visited [[Hawke's Bay]] to meet with local leaders and local National MPs [[Katie Nimon]] and [[Catherine Wedd]] to discuss government support for post-[[2023 Auckland Anniversary Weekend floods|flood]] and [[Cyclone Gabrielle]] relief efforts in the region.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Mitchell |first1=Mark |title=Ministers visit Hawke's Bay to grasp recovery needs |url=https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/ministers-visit-hawke%E2%80%99s-bay-grasp-recovery-needs |website=Beehive.govt.nz |publisher=[[New Zealand Government]] |access-date=21 December 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231213042708/https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/ministers-visit-hawke%E2%80%99s-bay-grasp-recovery-needs |archive-date=13 December 2023 |date=8 December 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Pocock-2023">{{cite news |last1=Pocock |first1=James |title=Christopher Luxon puts brakes on Napier-Wairoa rail during Hawke's Bay visit |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/hawkes-bay-today/news/christopher-luxon-puts-brakes-on-napier-wairoa-rail-during-hawkes-bay-visit/2FDU7SG7H5C5VOI7SZC4I5OCJ4/ |access-date=21 December 2023 |work=[[Hawke's Bay Today]] |date=8 December 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231217190800/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/hawkes-bay-today/news/christopher-luxon-puts-brakes-on-napier-wairoa-rail-during-hawkes-bay-visit/2FDU7SG7H5C5VOI7SZC4I5OCJ4/ |archive-date=17 December 2023}}</ref> During the visit, Luxon confirmed that the Government would pause work on restoring the [[Napier, New Zealand|Napier]]-[[Wairoa]] railway line to focus on repairing [[State Highway 2 (New Zealand)|State Highway 2]].<ref name="Pocock-2023" />

On 11 February 2024, Luxon and Emergency Management and Recovery Minister [[Mark Mitchell (New Zealand politician)|Mark Mitchell]] announced that the Government would contribute NZ$63 million to aid the removal of sediment and debris caused by Cyclone Gabrielle in the [[Hawke's Bay]] and [[Gisborne District]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Government announces $63 million cyclone recovery boost |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/02/11/government-announces-63-million-cyclone-recovery-boost/ |access-date=14 February 2024 |work=[[1News]] |date=11 February 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240214032512/https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/02/11/government-announces-63-million-cyclone-recovery-boost/ |archive-date=14 February 2024|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Cabinet reshuffles ===
In late April 2024, Luxon stripped [[Melissa Lee]] of her [[Minister for Media and Communications|Media and Communications ministerial portfolio]] and [[Penny Simmonds]] of her Disability Issues portfolio during a [[cabinet reshuffle]]. Lee had faced criticism for her response to [[Warner Bros. Discovery]]'s closure of ''[[Newshub]]'' while Simmonds had faced criticism over her handling of changes to disability funding and services. Lee was also relieved of her Cabinet position. Luxon appointed [[Paul Goldsmith (politician)|Paul Goldsmith]] to the Media and Communications portfolio, [[Louise Upston]] to the Disability Issues portfolio and promoted Climate Change and Revenue Minister [[Simon Watts]] to the Cabinet.<ref>{{cite news |title=Media Minister Melissa Lee demoted from Cabinet, Penny Simmonds stripped of portfolio |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/515100/media-minister-melissa-lee-demoted-from-cabinet-penny-simmonds-stripped-of-portfolio |access-date=24 April 2024 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=24 April 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240424032409/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/515100/media-minister-melissa-lee-demoted-from-cabinet-penny-simmonds-stripped-of-portfolio |archive-date=24 April 2024|url-status=live}}</ref>

Luxon initiated a second [[cabinet reshuffle]] on 19 January 2025. [[Simeon Brown]] gained the health portfolio from [[Shane Reti]] and also became [[Minister for State Owned Enterprises]]. Reti became [[Minister of Science, Innovation and Technology]] and also gained the new Universities portfolio. [[Chris Bishop]] gained the [[Minister of Transport (New Zealand)|transportation]] portfolio while Watts gained the [[Minister of Energy (New Zealand)|energy]] and [[Minister of Local Government (New Zealand)|local government]] portfolios. Lee lost her economic growth and ethnic communities ministerial portfolios to [[Nicola Willis]] and [[Mark Mitchell (New Zealand politician)|Mark Mitchell]] respectively. [[James Meager]] gained the hunting and fishing, [[Minister for Youth (New Zealand)|youth]], and the new [[South Island]] portfolios. [[Matt Doocey]] lost his [[Accident Compensation Corporation|ACC]], [[Minister of Tourism (New Zealand)|tourism]] and youth portfolios to [[Andrew Bayly]], Upston and Meager respectively. [[Chris Penk]] assumed the small business and manufacturing portfolio. No changes were made to ACT and New Zealand First ministerial portfolios.<ref>{{cite news |title=As it happened: Luxon announces Cabinet reshuffle, Shane Reti loses health portfolio to Simeon Brown |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/539370/as-it-happened-luxon-announces-cabinet-reshuffle-shane-reti-loses-health-portfolio-to-simeon-brown |access-date=19 January 2025 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=19 January 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250119031900/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/539370/as-it-happened-luxon-announces-cabinet-reshuffle-shane-reti-loses-health-portfolio-to-simeon-brown |archive-date=19 January 2025}}</ref> On 21 February, Bayly resigned from his [[Minister of Commerce and Consumer Affairs|Commerce and Consumer Affairs]] and ACC portfolios following allegations of "overbearing" behaviour. National's Senior Whip [[Scott Simpson (politician)|Scott Simpson]] assumed those portfolios.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ewe |first1=Koh |last2=Armstrong |first2=Kathryn |title=NZ minister resigns after he 'placed hand' on staff's arm |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/ce30l5497x2o |access-date=24 February 2025 |work=[[BBC News]] |date=24 February 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250302030202/https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/ce30l5497x2o|archive-date=2 March 2025|url-status=live}}</ref>

Luxon initiated a third major cabinet reshuffle in early April 2026 to address the planned departures of cabinet ministers [[Judith Collins]] and Reti prior to the [[2026 New Zealand general election]]. Penk joined Cabinet, gaining the defence, [[Government Communications Security Bureau]] (GCSB), [[New Zealand Security Intelligence Service]] (NZSIS) and space portfolios. [[Penny Simmonds]] rejoined Cabinet, gaining the tertiary education and science, innovation and technology portfolios. Chris Bishop became [[Attorney-General (New Zealand)|Attorney-General]] but lost the [[Leader of the House (New Zealand)|Leader of the House]] and campaign manager roles to [[Louise Upston]] and Simeon Brown respectively. Goldsmith also gained the Public Service, Digitising Government, and [[Minister for Pacific Peoples|Pacific Peoples]] portfolios.<ref name="1News 2 Apr 2026">{{cite news |title=Christopher Luxon reveals Cabinet shake-up |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/04/02/christopher-luxon-reveals-cabinet-shake-up/ |access-date=2 April 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=2 April 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260402141448/https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/04/02/christopher-luxon-reveals-cabinet-shake-up/ |archive-date=2 April 2026|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Beehive 2 Apr 2026">{{cite web |title=PM refreshes ministerial team |url=https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/pm-refreshes-ministerial-team |website=Beehive.govt.nz |publisher=[[New Zealand Government]] |access-date=3 April 2026 |date=2 April 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260403014312/https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/pm-refreshes-ministerial-team|archive-date=3 April 2026}}</ref>

=== Local governance ===
Luxon and the National Party had pledged to replace the controversial [[Three Waters reform programme]],<ref>{{cite web |title=National pledges to scrap Three Waters and 'deliver local water well' |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/484842/national-pledges-to-scrap-three-waters-and-deliver-local-water-well |website=RNZ |access-date=13 June 2026 |language=en-nz |date=25 February 2023}}</ref> and after taking office his government introduced [[Local Water Done Well]] as its alternative approach to managing water services.<ref>{{cite web |title=Labour's Three Waters legislation repealed |url=https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/labour%E2%80%99s-three-waters-legislation-repealed |website=Beehive.govt.nz |publisher=[[New Zealand Government]] |access-date=18 February 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240214062045/https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/labour%E2%80%99s-three-waters-legislation-repealed |archive-date=14 February 2024 |date=14 February 2024 |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 5 May 2024, Luxon, [[Minister of Local Government (New Zealand)|Local Government Minister]] [[Simeon Brown]] and [[Mayor of Auckland]] [[Wayne Brown (New Zealand politician)|Wayne Brown]] jointly announced that Auckland would avoid a 25.8 percent rates increase as part of the Government's Local Water Done Well plan.<ref>{{cite web |title=Local Water Done Well agreement flows for Auckland |url=https://insidegovernment.co.nz/local-water-done-well-agreement-flows-for-auckland/ |website=Inside Government |publisher=[[JSL Media]] |access-date=7 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240507212313/https://insidegovernment.co.nz/local-water-done-well-agreement-flows-for-auckland/ |archive-date=7 May 2024 |date=5 May 2024 |url-status=live}}</ref>

During [[Local Government New Zealand]]'s annual conference held on 21 August 2024, Luxon called on local and regional councils to focus on rubbish collection, water infrastructure and other local assets. He also criticised so-called "nice to have projects" such as the [[Tākina]] convention centre. Luxon also confirmed that Cabinet had agreed to revise the Local Government Act and scrap the four wellbeing provisions in that legislation.<ref>{{cite news |title=Local councils need to 'rein in the fantasies' on spending – PM |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/525788/local-councils-need-to-rein-in-the-fantasies-on-spending-pm |access-date=2 September 2024 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=27 August 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240827212448/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/525788/local-councils-need-to-rein-in-the-fantasies-on-spending-pm |archive-date=27 August 2024|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 23 June 2025, Luxon said he was open to abolishing [[Regional councils of New Zealand|regional councils]] as part of the Government's reform of the [[Resource Management Act 1991]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Pearse |first1=Adam |title=PM Christopher Luxon open to scrapping regional councils amid RMA reform |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/pm-christopher-luxon-open-to-scrapping-regional-councils-amid-rma-reform/4KP6H4R4Q5DN5I4WXSHKPRPO54/ |access-date=27 June 2025 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=23 June 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250623040459/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/pm-christopher-luxon-open-to-scrapping-regional-councils-amid-rma-reform/4KP6H4R4Q5DN5I4WXSHKPRPO54/ |archive-date=23 June 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Education ===
In early August 2024, Luxon and [[Minister of Education (New Zealand)|Education Minister]] [[Erica Stanford]] announced plans to introduce a new mathematics curriculum from early 2025 with a focus on raising student standards and achievement.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Luxon |first1=Chris |last2=Stanford |first2=Erica |title=Government transforms maths education |url=https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/government-transforms-maths-education |website=Beehive.govt.nz |publisher=[[New Zealand Government]] |access-date=7 August 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240804212838/https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/government-transforms-maths-education |archive-date=4 August 2024 |date=4 August 2024 |url-status=live}}</ref> In response, the [[New Zealand Educational Institute]] expressed concerns that rapid changes to the maths and literacy curriculum and the short teaching training timeframe would strain the teaching workforce without delivering on its goals.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Nightingale |first1=Melissa |title=Government's maths teaching changes not in line with expert recommendations, union says |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/governments-maths-teaching-changes-not-in-line-with-expert-recommendations-union-says/LMR2JHOEPJGJHFWQXDPR6FIZUI/ |access-date=7 August 2024 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=4 August 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240806130240/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/governments-maths-teaching-changes-not-in-line-with-expert-recommendations-union-says/LMR2JHOEPJGJHFWQXDPR6FIZUI/ |archive-date=6 August 2024|url-status=live}}</ref>

In early August 2025, Luxon and Stanford confirmed that the Government would scrap the [[National Certificate of Educational Achievement]] (NCEA) secondary school qualification over the next five years.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ensor |first1=Jamie |title=Live updates: Government to abolish NCEA in radical shake-up of main secondary school qualification |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/ncea-changes-live-updates-stanford-luxon-to-reveal-proposal-for-secondary-school-qualification/NH5UTCVJ4RF5ZAPLYQZDGTOSU4/ |access-date=4 August 2025 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=4 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250804010913/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/ncea-changes-live-updates-stanford-luxon-to-reveal-proposal-for-secondary-school-qualification/NH5UTCVJ4RF5ZAPLYQZDGTOSU4/ |archive-date=4 August 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Abuse survivors ===
On 10 November 2024, Luxon delivered the government's national apology to survivors of [[Royal Commission of Inquiry into Abuse in Care|abuse in state and faith-based care]] at Parliament.<ref>{{cite news |title=The full text of Christopher Luxon's Crown apology to abuse survivors |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/533547/the-full-text-of-christopher-luxon-s-crown-apology-to-abuse-survivors |access-date=12 November 2024 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=12 November 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241112031730/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/533547/the-full-text-of-christopher-luxon-s-crown-apology-to-abuse-survivors |archive-date=12 November 2024|url-status=live}}</ref> He also announced that the government was introducing legislation to combat abuse in care, including banning strip searches on children and strengthening security checks for people working with children.<ref>{{cite news |last=Pearse |first=Adam |title=Live updates: Christopher Luxon makes official apology for abuse in state care |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/abuse-in-state-care-government-cops-flak-as-redress-scheme-absent-ahead-of-apology/QTCLKHZYOBFANIPH4R7T6DSO3I/ |access-date=12 November 2024 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=12 November 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241112033545/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/abuse-in-state-care-government-cops-flak-as-redress-scheme-absent-ahead-of-apology/QTCLKHZYOBFANIPH4R7T6DSO3I/|archive-date=12 November 2024|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Social welfare ===
In 2022, Luxon stated National would make cuts to the [[Ministry of Social Development (New Zealand)|Ministry of Social Development]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Coughlan |first1=Thomas |date=7 August 2022 |title=National leader Chris Luxon on unemployment and delivery |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/national-leader-chris-luxon-on-unemployment-and-delivery/3TQ7HEBOQ5Z7ODNHVMOM7YEDFY/ |url-status=live |access-date=19 September 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220807041824/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/national-leader-chris-luxon-on-unemployment-and-delivery/3TQ7HEBOQ5Z7ODNHVMOM7YEDFY/ |archive-date=7 August 2022}}</ref>

In August 2024, Luxon and [[Louise Upston]], announced a "traffic light" regime for welfare was introduced, setting out escalating consequences for beneficiaries who fail to meet job-seeking or other obligations. From early 2025, jobseeker beneficiaries are required to reapply for their benefit every six months, instead of annually, and a new community work sanction was introduced requiring beneficiaries to "build skills and confidence."<ref>{{cite news |title=Watch: Government further increases sanctions for beneficiaries |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/524919/watch-government-further-increases-sanctions-for-beneficiaries |access-date=6 October 2025 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=12 August 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250911191829/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/524919/watch-government-further-increases-sanctions-for-beneficiaries |archive-date=11 September 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

In October 2025, Luxon and Upston confirmed that the Government would tighten welfare eligibility criteria for 18 and 19-year old teenagers; with those having parents earning over NZ$65,000 being ineligible for Jobseeker and other emergency benefits from November 2026. 18 and 19 year olds, with dependent children, or who were estranged from their parents would still be eligible for welfare assistance. He said: {{Blockquote|We're here to help and support as much as we can, but you also have to take responsibility for that and actually just consigning you to a life of welfare for 18 years is unacceptable.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dexter |first1=Giles |title=Jobseeker: Parents earning more than $65k must support 18-19yo children |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/575025/jobseeker-parents-earning-more-than-65k-must-support-18-19yo-children |access-date=6 October 2025 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=5 October 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251005041024/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/575025/jobseeker-parents-earning-more-than-65k-must-support-18-19yo-children |archive-date=5 October 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>}}

===Foreign affairs and defence===
[[File:2025 Donald Trump meeting with Prime Minister Christopher Luxon of New Zealand at the Hilton Gyeongju, South Korea on October 29 on the margins of APEC 2025.jpg|thumb|Luxon with American president [[Donald Trump]] in South Korea, 29 October 2025]]
[[File:Prime Minister Keir Starmer meets Christopher Luxon (54468990554).jpg|thumb|Luxon with British prime minister [[Keir Starmer]] in London, 22 April 2025]]
{{See also|List of international prime ministerial trips made by Christopher Luxon}}

According to ''[[The Economist]]'' and ''[[Foreign Policy]]'' magazine, New Zealand foreign policy under the National-led coalition government shifted away from China in favour of closer relations with its traditional [[Five Eyes]] partners, the United States, United Kingdom, Australia and Canada.<ref name="1News-2024">{{cite news |title=Govt's foreign policy stance huge 'pivot' – international media |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/10/23/govts-foreign-policy-stance-huge-pivot-international-media/ |access-date=27 October 2024 |work=[[1News]] |date=23 October 2024}}</ref> During an interview with ''The Economist'', Luxon said that he was looking to "diversify New Zealand's diplomatic and trade relationships away from its [[China-New Zealand relations|reliance]] on China." The magazine described this shift as New Zealand's biggest pivot since the [[ANZUS]] dispute in 1986 that was triggered by [[New Zealand nuclear-free zone|New Zealand's nuclear-free policy]].<ref>{{cite news |title=New Zealand's biggest pivot since the 1980s |url=https://www.economist.com/asia/2024/10/17/new-zealands-biggest-pivot-since-the-1980s |access-date=27 October 2024 |newspaper=[[The Economist]] |date=17 October 2024|url-access=subscription}}</ref><ref name="1News-2024" />
[[File:Fumio Kishida and Christopher Luxon at the Kantei 2024 (1).jpg|thumb|Luxon with Japanese prime minister [[Fumio Kishida]], 19 June 2024]]
''Foreign Policy'' columnist Derek Grossman wrote that Luxon and [[Minister of Foreign Affairs (New Zealand)|Foreign Minister]] [[Winston Peters]] were continuing a thaw in [[New Zealand-United States relations|New Zealand–United States relations]] that began under National prime minister [[John Key]] in 2010 and was continued by Labour prime minister [[Jacinda Ardern]] and [[Chris Hipkins]]. Notable examples of this pro-US shift included the [[Royal New Zealand Navy]] sending a ship to sail through the disputed [[Taiwan Strait]] and the National-led government's expressed interest in partnering with [[AUKUS]] and [[NATO]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Grossman |first1=Derek |title=New Zealand Becomes the Latest Country to Pivot to the U.S. |url=https://foreignpolicy.com/2024/04/23/new-zealand-us-china-alliances-nato-aukus/ |access-date=27 October 2024 |work=[[Foreign Policy]] |date=23 April 2024}}</ref>

In late August 2024, Luxon expressed support for [[Taiwan]]'s participation in the [[Pacific Islands Forum]] in response to Chinese plans to lobby for member states to exclude Taiwan from attending the Forum's 2025 event.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Pearse |first1=Adam |title=PM Christopher Luxon says New Zealand supports Taiwan's Pacific forum links amid report China pushing for exclusion |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/pm-christopher-luxon-says-new-zealand-supports-taiwans-pacific-forum-links-amid-report-china-pushing-for-exclusion/X3EMIC6IINGE5PWW77YSDZJI74/ |access-date=7 September 2024 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=29 August 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240906093601/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/pm-christopher-luxon-says-new-zealand-supports-taiwans-pacific-forum-links-amid-report-china-pushing-for-exclusion/X3EMIC6IINGE5PWW77YSDZJI74/ |archive-date=6 September 2024}}</ref>

In April 2025, Luxon announced the Government will allocate $12 billion to defence spending over the next four years. Luxon said it was time New Zealand "pulls its weight" internationally. This will bring New Zealand's spending on defence up to 2% of its GDP, up from 1%.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Smith |first=Sam |date=7 April 2025 |title=Government hikes defence spending amid 'tectonic shifts' in geopolitics |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360644298/government-hikes-defence-spending-amid-tectonic-shifts-geopolitics |access-date=7 August 2026 |website=Stuff |language=en}}</ref>

==== Australia ====
On 20 December 2023, Luxon made his first state visit as head of government to [[Sydney]] where he met [[Australian Prime Minister|Australian prime minister]] [[Anthony Albanese]]. During his visit, Luxon affirmed New Zealand's [[New Zealand nuclear-free zone|nuclear-free policy]] but expressed openness to joining the non-nuclear pillar of the [[AUKUS]] agreement, but also stated that New Zealand was keen to do its share of "heavy lifting" in the Australia–New Zealand defence relationship. Luxon and Albanese also talked about the contribution of [[New Zealand Australians]] to Australia.<ref name="Dexter-2023">{{cite news |last1=Dexter |first1=Giles |title=Luxon 'exploring' non-nuclear part of AUKUS pact |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/505227/luxon-exploring-non-nuclear-part-of-aukus-pact |access-date=21 December 2023 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=21 December 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231221011514/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/505227/luxon-exploring-non-nuclear-part-of-aukus-pact |archive-date=21 December 2023|url-status=live}}</ref>

Between 9 and 10 August 2025, Luxon hosted Albanese in [[Queenstown, New Zealand|Queenstown]] for the annual bilateral head of government talks.<ref>{{cite news |title=Luxon and Albanese discuss 'geopolitical tensions' during annual talk |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2025/08/09/luxon-and-albanese-discuss-geopolitical-tensions-during-annual-talk/ |access-date=11 August 2025 |work=[[1News]] |date=9 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250810235116/https://www.1news.co.nz/2025/08/09/luxon-and-albanese-discuss-geopolitical-tensions-during-annual-talk/ |archive-date=10 August 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> They discussed various trade and defence issues, the [[Gaza war]] and Australia's [[Migration Act 1958#2014: Character test|501 deportation policy]].<ref name="Roxburgh-2025">{{cite news |last1=Roxburgh |first1=Tracy |title=Luxon, Albanese make the most of Q'town |url=https://www.odt.co.nz/regions/queenstown/luxon-albanese-make-most-q%E2%80%99town |access-date=11 August 2025 |work=[[Otago Daily Times]] |date=11 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250811001001/https://www.odt.co.nz/regions/queenstown/luxon-albanese-make-most-q%E2%80%99town |archive-date=11 August 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Corlett-2025">{{cite news |last1=Corlett |first1=Eva |title=Cuddles, cuppas and chemistry: Albanese and Luxon downplay divisions in times of global turmoil |url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/aug/10/cuddles-cuppas-and-chemistry-albanese-and-luxon-downplay-divisions-in-times-of-global-turmoil |access-date=11 August 2025 |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=10 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250810235233/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/aug/10/cuddles-cuppas-and-chemistry-albanese-and-luxon-downplay-divisions-in-times-of-global-turmoil |archive-date=10 August 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>  Other local and civil society leaders attended the meeting including [[Mayor of Queenstown-Lakes]] Glyn Lewers, [[Southland (New Zealand electorate)|Southland]] MP [[Joseph Mooney (New Zealand politician)|Joseph Mooney]], [[Ngai Tahu]] [[Tipene O'Regan]] and Edward Ellison, and New Zealand actor and film-maker [[Cliff Curtis]].<ref name="Roxburgh-2025" /> The two leaders laid wreaths at a memorial to fallen [[ANZAC]] soldiers in [[Arrowtown]] on 10 August.<ref name="Corlett-2025" />

Luxon has consistently expressed strong opposition to Australia's 501 deportation policy, particularly when it results in people with minimal ties to New Zealand being deported. He has repeatedly called the policy "regrettable" and "not right." He has expressed disappointment that Australia reversed its previous, more lenient approach, which took into account the length of time someone had lived in Australia and their family connections before deporting them.<ref>{{cite news |last=Trevett |first=Claire |title=PM Christopher Luxon'regrets' Australia's move to harden 501s deportations policy |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/pm-christopher-luxon-regrets-australias-move-to-harden-501s-deportations-policy/WKQWB7MBHJHIPCBWAQDTPVTTUU/ |access-date=11 August 2025 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=7 June 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250615153021/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/pm-christopher-luxon-regrets-australias-move-to-harden-501s-deportations-policy/WKQWB7MBHJHIPCBWAQDTPVTTUU/ |archive-date=15 June 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> He has stated, "It's just not right that people who have no connection to New Zealand are deported to New Zealand."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Sowmand-Lund |first1=Stewart |title=Australia's hardline deportation policy and what it means for the government |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/the-bulletin/12-06-2024/australias-hardline-deportation-policy-and-what-it-means-for-the-government |access-date=11 August 2025 |work=[[The Spinoff]] |date=12 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250624072035/https://thespinoff.co.nz/the-bulletin/12-06-2024/australias-hardline-deportation-policy-and-what-it-means-for-the-government |archive-date=24 June 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Following his discussions with Albanese, Luxon said: "We respect the fact that Australia has its own policies around deportation," but hoped that common sense would prevail.<ref name="Dexter-2023"/>

=== Coalition dynamics ===
Luxon's leadership and ability to govern effectively have been challenged by the roles and actions of his coalition partners, [[David Seymour]] (ACT) and [[Winston Peters]] (NZ First). A recurring concern is his perceived lack of control over his coalition partners.<ref name="Luxon-2">{{cite web |last1=Rashbrooke |first1=Max |title=Luxon's epic unpopularity in one chart |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/17-03-2025/luxons-epic-unpopularity-in-one-chart |website=[[The Spinoff]] |access-date=29 April 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260421030629/https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/17-03-2025/luxons-epic-unpopularity-in-one-chart |archive-date=21 April 2026 |date=17 March 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> A poll in April 2024 found that 51% of respondents say Luxon is the decision maker in government.<ref name="P Poll">{{cite news |title=Poll: Only 51% say Luxon is the decision-maker in coalition govt |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/04/30/poll-only-51-say-luxon-is-the-decision-maker-in-coalition-govt/ |access-date=29 April 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=30 April 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250726093645/https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/04/30/poll-only-51-say-luxon-is-the-decision-maker-in-coalition-govt/ |archive-date=26 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

Seymour has openly challenged Luxon's authority, particularly around contentious issues like the Treaty Principles Bill.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wilson |first1=Sue |title=Luxon's leadership test: what would it take to win back unimpressed NZ voters? |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/on-the-inside/515753/luxon-s-leadership-test-what-would-it-take-to-win-back-unimpressed-nz-voters |access-date=29 April 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |agency=[[The Conversation (website)|The Conversation]] |date=2 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250718211523/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/on-the-inside/515753/luxon-s-leadership-test-what-would-it-take-to-win-back-unimpressed-nz-voters |archive-date=18 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Seymour has even asserted that Luxon cannot unilaterally sack ACT ministers.<ref name="P Poll"/> Peters has not hesitated to critique government policy or his own coalition's fiscal plans. In March 2024, he undercut finance minister [[Nicola Willis]] by claiming a $5.6b fiscal hole would result from her intended tax cuts.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wilson |first1=Peter |title=Week in Politics: Fiscal bomb, Nazi comments ensure high-octane coverage for Winston Peters |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/on-the-inside/512406/week-in-politics-fiscal-bomb-nazi-comments-ensure-high-octane-coverage-for-winston-peters |access-date=29 April 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=22 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240528020952/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/on-the-inside/512406/week-in-politics-fiscal-bomb-nazi-comments-ensure-high-octane-coverage-for-winston-peters |archive-date=28 May 2024 |url-status=live}}</ref>

===National Party leadership===
Luxon's background as a corporate executive is often cited as influencing his communication style. He has been criticised for using business jargon, such as referring to voters as "customers."<ref>{{cite news |title=PM Christopher Luxon admits he needs to 'work harder on corporate speak' after 'customers' reference |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/in-depth/532729/pm-christopher-luxon-admits-he-needs-to-work-harder-on-corporate-speak-after-customers-reference |access-date=29 April 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=4 November 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260221160317/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/in-depth/532729/pm-christopher-luxon-admits-he-needs-to-work-harder-on-corporate-speak-after-customers-reference |archive-date=21 February 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Dunne |first1=Peter |title=Luxon's Political Communication Challenge |url=https://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/HL2412/S00005/luxons-political-communication-challenge.htm |website=[[Scoop (website)|Scoop]] |access-date=29 April 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250812064007/https://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/HL2412/S00005/luxons-political-communication-challenge.htm |archive-date=12 August 2025 |date=4 December 2024 |url-status=live}}</ref>

In April 2024, a ''[[1News]]''-[[Verian]] [[Opinion polling for the next New Zealand general election|poll]] showed the National–ACT–New Zealand First [[Sixth National Government of New Zealand|coalition government]] convincingly behind the left bloc of [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]], the [[Green Party of Aotearoa New Zealand|Green Party]] and [[Te Pāti Māori]]. It also cast doubt on the strength of Luxon's leadership compared to that of his coalition partners Winston Peters and David Seymour, with 51% of respondents believing Luxon was the decision-maker in the government.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2 May 2024 |title=Luxon's leadership test: what would it take to win back unimpressed NZ voters? |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/515753/luxon-s-leadership-test-what-would-it-take-to-win-back-unimpressed-nz-voters |access-date=17 June 2024 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]]}}</ref>

A subsequent poll by Talbot Mills suggested a Labour-led government would have a bare majority of 62 seats, while Roy Morgan found 54% of those they surveyed believed New Zealand was heading in "the wrong direction", with just 35% believing it was in the right direction.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Poll: Labour could return to power if election held today |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/04/29/poll-labour-could-return-to-power-if-election-held-today/ |access-date=17 June 2024 |work=[[1News]] |archive-date=11 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240511090809/https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/04/29/poll-labour-could-return-to-power-if-election-held-today/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=18 June 2024 |title=Political Roundup: Discontent grips NZ amid dark economic times |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/political-roundup-discontent-and-gloom-dominate-nzs-political-mood/BJONTIXBCJHT7M4XLWZAWWUY7U/ |access-date=17 June 2024 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |language=en-NZ |archive-date=8 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240508075707/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/political-roundup-discontent-and-gloom-dominate-nzs-political-mood/BJONTIXBCJHT7M4XLWZAWWUY7U/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Has Christopher Luxon caught the incumbency curse? |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/350261476/has-christopher-luxon-caught-incumbency-curse |access-date=17 June 2024 |website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=30 April 2024 |archive-date=17 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240617141753/https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/350261476/has-christopher-luxon-caught-incumbency-curse |url-status=live }}</ref> In June 2024, a poll showed Luxon fall behind opposition leader [[Chris Hipkins]] in net favourability for the first time.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Major parties lose support in new poll, support for Luxon plummets |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2024/06/national-labour-lose-support-in-new-poll-support-for-christopher-luxon-plummets.html |access-date=17 June 2024 |work=[[Newshub]] |archive-date=17 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240617141752/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2024/06/national-labour-lose-support-in-new-poll-support-for-christopher-luxon-plummets.html |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Opinion polls showed that Luxon's lack of popularity deepened over his first 18 months in office. His net favourability was notably lower than previous first-term Prime Ministers such as Helen Clark, John Key, and Jacinda Ardern. Six months later, in October 2025, The Post/Freshwater Strategy poll found that 27% of voters viewed Luxon positively, while 51% had an unfavourable view.<ref>[https://www.thepost.co.nz/politics/360853311/big-names-risk-labour-pulls-ahead-and-christopher-luxon-loses-ground Big names at risk as Labour pulls ahead and Christopher Luxon loses ground], The Post, 15 October 2025</ref>

Luxon's leadership of the party has repeatedly come under scrutiny. In November 2025, [[Chris Bishop]] was named as the prospective instigator of a potential plot to oust Luxon as leader, but the plan never came to fruition.<ref>{{Cite web |last=McGregor |first=Catherine |date=1 December 2025 |title=Luxon, Bishop and the coup that never happened |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/the-bulletin/01-12-2025/luxon-bishop-and-the-coup-that-never-happened |access-date=13 August 2026 |website=The Spinoff}}</ref> Successive [[Opinion polling for the 2026 New Zealand general election|poor polling]] throughout April 2026 and speculation on his suitability as leader led to Luxon calling a vote of confidence in his leadership at a party caucus meeting on 21 April. The motion passed,<ref>{{cite news |last=Saunokonoko |first=Mark |title=Embattled New Zealand prime minister survives leadership vote and blames media for 'soap opera' |url= https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/apr/21/new-zealand-prime-minister-christopher-luxon-leadership-vote |access-date=29 April 2026 |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=20 April 2026 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260423052106/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/apr/21/new-zealand-prime-minister-christopher-luxon-leadership-vote |archive-date=23 April 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|last=Dexter |first=Giles |date=21 April 2026 |title=Prime Minister Christopher Luxon survives confidence vote |url= https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/592984/prime-minister-christopher-luxon-survives-confidence-vote |access-date=22 April 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260421210959/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/592984/prime-minister-christopher-luxon-survives-confidence-vote |archive-date=21 April 2026|url-status=live}}</ref> reportedly by 27–22.<ref>{{Cite web |date=13 August 2026 |title=Christopher Luxon insists he took 'decisive action' with Chris Penk's leadership challenge |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/992072/christopher-luxon-insists-he-took-decisive-action-with-chris-penk-s-leadership-challenge |access-date=13 August 2026 |website=RNZ}}</ref> The speculation became prominent again later that year. On 11 August 2026, Luxon abruptly called [[August 2026 New Zealand National Party leadership election|another vote of confidence in himself]] for the next day.<ref>{{Cite web |date=11 August 2026 |title=Christopher Luxon refuses to speak to media as he arrives in Wellington ahead of urgent leadership meeting |url= https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/964763/christopher-luxon-refuses-to-speak-to-media-as-he-arrives-in-wellington-ahead-of-urgent-leadership-meeting |access-date=11 August 2026 |website=RNZ}}</ref> Minister of Defence [[Chris Penk]] emerged as his challenger. Luxon defeated Penk and purged him from cabinet.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Clark |first=Poppy |date=13 August 2026 |title=How National's secret vote works after Chris Penk's failed leadership bid |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/361019447/how-nationals-secret-vote-works-after-chris-penks-failed-leadership-bid |access-date=13 August 2026 |website=Stuff}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=12 August 2026 |title=Chris Penk says he will not seek re-election after losing ministerial portfolios |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/975747/chris-penk-says-he-will-not-seek-re-election-after-losing-ministerial-portfolios |access-date=13 August 2026 |website=RNZ}}</ref>

==Political positions==
{{Quote box
| quote = My faith is personal to me. It is not in itself a political agenda. I believe no religion should dictate to the State, and no politician should use the political platform they have to force their beliefs on others. As MPs, we serve the common cause of all New Zealanders—not one religion, not one group, not one interest. A person should not be elected because of their faith, nor should they be rejected because of it. 
| source = – Luxon's maiden speech,  24 March 2021
| width = 27em
| quoted = yes
}}
Luxon is an [[Evangelicalism|evangelical Christian]] who is recognised as a [[social conservative]].<ref>{{cite news |last1= Wade |first1= Amelia |date= 30 November 2021 |title= Who is the new National Party leader Christopher Luxon? |work=[[Newshub]] |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2021/11/who-is-the-new-national-party-leader-christopher-luxon.html |access-date= 27 September 2023 |archive-date= 2 October 2023 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20231002105711/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2021/11/who-is-the-new-national-party-leader-christopher-luxon.html |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Shaw |first= Richard |date= 12 September 2023 |title= After the election, Christopher Luxon's real test could come from his right – not the left |url=http://theconversation.com/after-the-election-christopher-luxons-real-test-could-come-from-his-right-not-the-left-209393 |access-date=1 October 2023 |website=[[The Conversation (website)|The Conversation]] |language=en |archive-date=10 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231010035420/http://theconversation.com/after-the-election-christopher-luxons-real-test-could-come-from-his-right-not-the-left-209393 |url-status=live }}</ref> In his [[maiden parliamentary speech]], Luxon defined himself as [[centre-right]] and moderate;<ref name="Luxon" /> the government he leads has been described as [[Conservatism|conservative]] and [[Right-wing politics|right-wing]], and one of the most so since [[Fourth National Government of New Zealand|the 1990s]].<ref>{{Cite news|last1=Malpass  |first1=Luke |title=Kiwis voted for a right-wing government – now they will get it|url=https://www.thepost.co.nz/politics/350118294/kiwis-voted-right-wing-government-now-they-will-get-it |access-date=5 March 2024 |work=[[The Post (newspaper)|The Post]] |archive-date=10 March 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240310022800/https://www.thepost.co.nz/politics/350118294/kiwis-voted-right-wing-government-now-they-will-get-it |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Hooton |first=Matthew |date=29 November 2023 |title=Kiwi coalition marks return to the right |url=https://www.theaustralian.com.au/commentary/kiwi-coalition-marks-return-to-the-right/news-story/2030a9d7d2689c582372e9014022e0cc |access-date=6 March 2024 |work=[[The Australian]]|url-access=subscription}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Cooke |first=Henry |date=4 November 2023 |title=Bumpy roads ahead: New Zealand's incoming PM set to lead a three-headed, 'anti-woke' government |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/nov/04/bumpy-roads-ahead-new-zealands-incoming-pm-set-to-lead-a-three-headed-anti-woke-government |access-date=5 March 2024 |work=[[The Guardian]] |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |agency=[[Associated Press]] |date=14 October 2023 |title=New Zealand elects conservative Christopher Luxon as premier after 6 years of liberal rule |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2023/10/14/new-zealand-elects-conservative-christopher-luxon-as-premier-after-6-years-of-liberal-rule-00121586 |access-date=6 March 2024 |website=Politico |archive-date=29 March 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240329202749/https://www.politico.com/news/2023/10/14/new-zealand-elects-conservative-christopher-luxon-as-premier-after-6-years-of-liberal-rule-00121586 |url-status=live }}</ref> Luxon supports low taxes,<ref>{{cite news |last=Coughlan |first=Thomas |title=Election 2023: National's Christopher Luxon and Nicola Willis promise $14.6b in tax cuts, Labour slams 'voodoo costings' |url= https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/election-2023-nationals-christopher-luxon-and-nicola-willis-promise-146b-in-tax-cuts-labour-slams-voodoo-costings/2R3XD4OYJVGERO4IM2THFIWTTA/ |access-date=5 March 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=31 August 2023 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |title= Christopher Luxon defends National tax policy after analysis finds for every $1 bottom half of earners get back, top 5 pct get $10 |language=en |work=[[Newshub]] |url= https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/11/christopher-luxon-defends-national-tax-policy-after-analysis-finds-for-every-1-bottom-half-of-earners-get-back-top-5-pct-get-10.html |access-date=4 July 2023 |archive-date=4 July 2023 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20230704020715/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/11/christopher-luxon-defends-national-tax-policy-after-analysis-finds-for-every-1-bottom-half-of-earners-get-back-top-5-pct-get-10.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> making cuts to the [[Ministry of Social Development (New Zealand)|Ministry of Social Development]],<ref>{{Cite news |title=Christopher Luxon explains his 'bottom feeding' comments |url= https://www.1news.co.nz/2022/03/22/christopher-luxon-explains-his-bottom-feeding-comments/ |access-date= 4 July 2023 |work=[[1News]] |language=en |archive-date=4 September 2023 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20230904215124/https://www.1news.co.nz/2022/03/22/christopher-luxon-explains-his-bottom-feeding-comments/ |url-status= live }}</ref> establishing military-style [[Boot camp (correctional)|boot-camp]]s for young offenders, and introducing stringent anti-[[Gangs in New Zealand|gang]] legislation, and is opposed to [[welfare dependency]] and to [[co-governance]] with [[Māori people|Māori]].<ref>Multiple sources:
*{{cite news |title=National commits to tough new anti-gang laws if elected |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2022/06/11/national-commits-to-tough-new-anti-gang-laws-if-elected/ |access-date=27 September 2023 |work=[[1News]] |date=11 June 2022 |archive-date=11 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220611021427/https://www.1news.co.nz/2022/06/11/national-commits-to-tough-new-anti-gang-laws-if-elected/ |url-status=live }}
*{{cite news |title=Gang membership would be aggravating factor in sentencing under new National policy |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/492210/gang-membership-would-be-aggravating-factor-in-sentencing-under-new-national-policy |access-date=27 September 2023 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=18 June 2023 |archive-date=18 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230618102719/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/492210/gang-membership-would-be-aggravating-factor-in-sentencing-under-new-national-policy |url-status=live}}
*{{cite web |title=Backing Police, Tackling Gangs |url=https://assets.nationbuilder.com/nationalparty/pages/17863/attachments/original/1693518597/Backing_police__tackling_gangs.pdf?1693518597 |publisher=New Zealand National Party |access-date=27 September 2023 |archive-date=27 September 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230927151709/https://assets.nationbuilder.com/nationalparty/pages/17863/attachments/original/1693518597/Backing_police__tackling_gangs.pdf?1693518597 |url-status=live }}
*{{Cite news |title=Rātana: Luxon says co-governance debate 'divisive and immature' |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2023/01/24/ratana-luxon-says-co-governance-debate-divisive-and-immature/ |access-date=4 July 2023 |work=[[1News]] |language=en |archive-date=4 July 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230704085316/https://www.1news.co.nz/2023/01/24/ratana-luxon-says-co-governance-debate-divisive-and-immature/ |url-status=live}}
*{{cite news |last1=Palmer |first1=Russell |title=Youth crime boot camps: National proposes military academies, electronic monitoring |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/478917/youth-crime-boot-camps-national-proposes-military-academies-electronic-monitoring |access-date=27 September 2023 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=17 November 2022 |archive-date=13 September 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230913022234/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/478917/youth-crime-boot-camps-national-proposes-military-academies-electronic-monitoring |url-status=live}}
*{{cite news |last1=McConnell |first1=Glenn |title=Christoper Luxon calls co-governance a 'divisive and immature conversation' |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300791183/christoper-luxon-calls-cogovernance-a-divisive-and-immature-conversation |access-date=27 September 2023 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=24 January 2023 |archive-date=27 September 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230927151705/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300791183/christoper-luxon-calls-cogovernance-a-divisive-and-immature-conversation |url-status=live}}</ref>

In November 2019, Luxon said he was against [[Abortion in New Zealand|abortion]], [[Euthanasia in New Zealand|euthanasia]], and [[Cannabis in New Zealand|legalising recreational cannabis]], though at the same time he supported [[Cannabis in New Zealand#Medicinal use|medicinal use of cannabis]].<ref>{{cite news |last1= Seah |first1=Naomii |title=The National Party's new conscience |url= https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/29-11-2021/the-national-partys-new-conscience |work=[[The Spinoff]] |date=29 November 2021 |access-date=1 December 2021 |archive-date=30 November 2021 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20211130105021/https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/29-11-2021/the-national-partys-new-conscience |url-status= live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title = Christopher Luxon suggests extension of 'no jab, no pay' policy |url= https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/402555/christopher-luxon-suggests-extension-of-no-jab-no-pay-policy |access-date = 10 August 2020 |work = [[Radio New Zealand]] |date = 5 November 2019 | quote = [...] Mr Luxon said he was against voluntary euthanasia, recreational cannabis and abortion reform – but is in favour of medicinal cannabis.|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200730212103/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/402555/christopher-luxon-suggests-extension-of-no-jab-no-pay-policy|archive-date=30 July 2020|url-status=live}}</ref> He also at the time supported a "no jab, no pay" policy for sanctioning welfare beneficiaries who did not vaccinate their children;<ref>{{cite news |last1= Cooke |first1= Henry |title= Christopher Luxon backs no-jab-no-pay further than the National Party |url= https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/117169551/christopher-luxon-backs-nojabnopay-further-the-national-party |access-date= 10 August 2020 |work= [[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date= 5 November 2019 |quote= He was asked by RNZ's [[Susie Ferguson]] whether he supported the policy of withdrawing sole parent support benefits for parents that don't vaccinate their children – currently just a 'proposal' by National, not an actual policy.<br />Luxon said he supported the idea, and even indicated some support for it extending to [[Working For Families]] benefits.|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20191110091330/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/117169551/christopher-luxon-backs-nojabnopay-further-the-national-party|archive-date=10 November 2019|url-status=live}}</ref> however, following his election as leader of the National Party, Luxon said he did not support cutting the benefits of parents who do not vaccinate their children against [[COVID-19 pandemic|COVID-19]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Christopher Luxon suggests National Party wants bipartisan housing accord|url= https://www.rnz.co.nz/audio/player?audio_id=2018822755|access-date= 30 November 2021|date=30 November 2021|website=[[Radio New Zealand]] |archive-date=30 November 2021|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20211130204751/https://www.rnz.co.nz/audio/player?audio_id=2018822755|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Abortion===
Luxon's views on abortion received media attention following his election as National's leader. He confirmed that his personal views are [[anti-abortion]], but said National will not contest the [[Abortion Legislation Act 2020]] should he become prime minister.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Ensor|first=Jamie|title=Christopher Luxon rules out changing abortion laws if he becomes Prime Minister|work=[[Newshub]]|url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2021/12/christopher-luxon-rules-out-changing-abortion-laws-if-he-becomes-prime-minister.html|access-date=30 November 2021|date=30 November 2021|archive-date=30 November 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211130190520/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2021/12/christopher-luxon-rules-out-changing-abortion-laws-if-he-becomes-prime-minister.html|url-status=dead}}</ref> After previously declining to answer when asked if he believes abortion to be tantamount to murder,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cooke |first1=Henry |title=Christopher Luxon says he will vote for safe zones outside abortion clinics at second reading |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300467250/christopher-luxon-says-he-will-vote-for-safe-zones-outside-abortion-clinics-at-second-reading |access-date=1 December 2021 |publisher=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=1 December 2021 |archive-date=30 November 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211130231451/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300467250/christopher-luxon-says-he-will-vote-for-safe-zones-outside-abortion-clinics-at-second-reading |url-status=live }}</ref> he said in an interview with ''[[Newshub]]'' that he is "a pro-life person," and when asked again about the murder comparison, he responded "that's what a pro-life position is."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lynch |first1=Jenna |title=Full interview: National leader Christopher Luxon and deputy leader Nicola Willis |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2021/12/full-interview-national-leader-christopher-luxon-and-deputy-leader-nicola-willis.html |access-date=1 December 2021 |work=[[Newshub]] |date=1 December 2021 |archive-date=1 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211201081407/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2021/12/full-interview-national-leader-christopher-luxon-and-deputy-leader-nicola-willis.html |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Lal |first=Shaneel |date=13 August 2023 |title=Shaneel Lal: Election 2023: Replace Christopher Luxon with Nicola Willis to take advantage of Labour's losses |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/shaneel-lal-replace-christopher-luxon-with-nicola-willis-to-take-advantage-of-labours-losses/ZLMLGAEQ7RHP7LXSXZTCF5ZTRY/ |access-date=2 October 2023 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |archive-date=10 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231010035419/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/shaneel-lal-replace-christopher-luxon-with-nicola-willis-to-take-advantage-of-labours-losses/ZLMLGAEQ7RHP7LXSXZTCF5ZTRY/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Gaza war===
In response to the [[Gaza war]], Luxon stated that he was shocked and saddened by [[Hamas]]'s [[October 7 attacks]] against Israel. Luxon condemned Hamas' attacks and stated that Israel had a right to defend itself.<ref>{{cite news |title=NZ leaders respond to Gaza attacks |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/live-updates/08-10-2023/nz-leaders-respond-to-gaza-attacks |access-date=10 October 2023 |work=[[The Spinoff]] |date=8 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231008042537/https://thespinoff.co.nz/live-updates/08-10-2023/nz-leaders-respond-to-gaza-attacks |archive-date=8 October 2023|url-status=dead}}</ref>

He later committed New Zealand to active military support, saying "I want us to be in lockstep with our partners who have common interests and actually be right there with them at that time."<ref name="Sachdeva-2024">{{cite news |last1=Sachdeva |first1=Sam |title=Luxon moves NZ in 'lockstep' on Red Sea |url=https://newsroom.co.nz/2024/01/24/luxon-moves-nz-in-lockstep-on-red-sea/ |access-date=2 February 2024 |work=Newsroom |date=23 January 2024 |archive-date=2 February 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240202213803/https://newsroom.co.nz/2024/01/24/luxon-moves-nz-in-lockstep-on-red-sea/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In January 2024, he authorised deployment of 6 NZDF personnel to support military action against [[Houthi movement|Houthi]] forces in the [[Red Sea]], saying "it's about us standing up for things that we believe in, and we can either talk about them or we can actually do something about it as well and make sure that we put real capability alongside our words".<ref name="Sachdeva-2024"/>

On 30 January 2024, Luxon announced that New Zealand would be suspending its annual NZ$1 million aid to [[UNRWA]] (the United Nations Relief and Works Agency for Palestinian Refugees in the Near East) following [[UNRWA October 7 controversy|allegations]] that several UNRWA workers had participated in the 7 October attacks.<ref>{{cite news |title=NZ halts funding for beleaguered UN aid agency |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/507907/nz-halts-funding-for-beleaguered-un-aid-agency |access-date=29 January 2024 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=30 January 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240129212225/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/507907/nz-halts-funding-for-beleaguered-un-aid-agency |archive-date=29 January 2024}}</ref> His announcement followed a decision by the [[Ministry of Foreign Affairs and Trade (New Zealand)|Ministry of Foreign Affairs and Trade]] to review New Zealand's aid to UNRWA.<ref>{{cite news |title=MFAT to review funding for under-fire UN agency accused over Hamas attacks |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/world/507826/mfat-to-review-funding-for-under-fire-un-agency-accused-over-hamas-attacks |access-date=29 January 2024 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=29 January 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240129045034/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/world/507826/mfat-to-review-funding-for-under-fire-un-agency-accused-over-hamas-attacks |archive-date=29 January 2024}}</ref> On 29 February 2024, Luxon announced New Zealand's designation of [[Hamas]] in its entirety as a terrorist entity.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/nz-designates-entirety-hamas-terrorist-entity |title=NZ designates entirety of Hamas as terrorist entity |date=29 February 2024 |publisher=New Zealand Government |access-date=29 February 2024 |archive-date=29 February 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240229115646/https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/nz-designates-entirety-hamas-terrorist-entity |url-status=live }}</ref>

As the war on Gaza unfolded, Luxon's position changed. On 2 December 2024, Luxon confirmed that New Zealand would comply with an [[International Criminal Court]] (ICC) [[International Criminal Court arrest warrants for Israeli leaders|arrest warrant]] in the event that [[Prime Minister of Israel|Israeli Prime Minister]] [[Benjamin Netanyahu]] visited New Zealand. During a press conference, he stated, "We believe in the international rules-based system, we support the ICC, and we would be obligated to do so."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Copek |first1=Caron |title=Christopher Luxon says Israel's PM Benjamin Netanyahu would be arrested if he came to NZ |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/nz-news/360507828/christopher-luxon-says-israels-pm-benjamin-netanyahu-would-be-arrested-if-he-came-nz |access-date=15 December 2024 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=2 December 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241205212736/https://www.stuff.co.nz/nz-news/360507828/christopher-luxon-says-israels-pm-benjamin-netanyahu-would-be-arrested-if-he-came-nz |archive-date=5 December 2024}}</ref> In June 2025, he described New Zealand's sanctions on Israeli ministers [[Bezalel Smotrich]] and [[Itamar Ben-Gvir]] as "entirely appropriate".<ref>{{cite web |title='Entirely appropriate': Luxon on NZ sanctions against Israeli ministers RNZ|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YqWlQb2t2sA |website=youtube.com |publisher=[[Radio New Zealand]] |language=en |format=video |date=11 June 2025}}</ref>

On 9 August 2025, Luxon and Australian Prime Minister [[Anthony Albanese]] called for a ceasefire in Gaza and opposed Israeli plans to occupy [[Gaza City]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Manfield |first1=Evelyn |title=Uncertainty in the Pacific and Middle East dominates Anthony Albanese's talks in NZ |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-08-09/anthony-albanese-new-zealand-visit-queenstown-anzac-force/105632892 |access-date=11 August 2025 |work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]] |date=9 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250811010732/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-08-09/anthony-albanese-new-zealand-visit-queenstown-anzac-force/105632892 |archive-date=11 August 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 13 August 2025, Luxon made remarks in Parliament criticising Israel's conduct during the Gaza war, describing it as "utterly, utterly appalling." He also said that "I think Netanyahu has gone way too far. I think he has lost the plot."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dexter |first1=Giles |title=War in Gaza: Israeli minister rejects Luxon's 'lost the plot' claim |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/569943/war-in-gaza-israeli-minister-rejects-luxon-s-lost-the-plot-claim |access-date=13 August 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=14 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250813235219/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/569943/war-in-gaza-israeli-minister-rejects-luxon-s-lost-the-plot-claim |archive-date=13 August 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

===COVID-19 pandemic===
Although Luxon frequently criticised the Labour Government's strategy for managing the [[COVID-19 pandemic]], on 8 January 2022, he stated that the National Party strongly supports [[COVID-19 vaccination]], and described vaccination as the best protection for people and their families.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Palmer |first1=Scott |title=COVID-19: National MP Harete Hipango deletes post showing her at another anti-vaccine mandate and lockdown protest |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/01/covid-19-national-mp-harete-hipango-deletes-post-showing-her-at-another-anti-vaccine-mandate-and-lockdown-protest.html |access-date=8 January 2022 |work=[[Newshub]] |date=8 January 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220108115323/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/01/covid-19-national-mp-harete-hipango-deletes-post-showing-her-at-another-anti-vaccine-mandate-and-lockdown-protest.html |archive-date=8 January 2022|url-status=dead}}</ref>

===Defence and security===
In response to US president [[Donald Trump]]'s announcement during the [[2025 APEC Summit]] that the United States would resume [[nuclear testing]], Luxon expressed disapproval of US plans to resume nuclear testing and reiterated his commitment to New Zealand's [[New Zealand nuclear-free zone|nuclear-free policy]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=Christopher Luxon meets new Japanese PM, other world leaders |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/577469/christopher-luxon-meets-new-japanese-pm-other-world-leaders |access-date=1 November 2025 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=1 November 2025}}</ref>

===Extending parliamentary term===
On 20 September 2024, Luxon said that the coalition government was open to holding a referendum to extend the parliamentary term from three to four years at the 2026 general election.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Walton |first1=Felix |title=Referendum on four-year political terms may come by next election – Luxon |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/528560/referendum-on-four-year-political-terms-may-come-by-next-election-luxon |access-date=21 September 2024 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=20 September 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240920164310/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/528567/resource-management-act-to-be-replaced-by-two-new-laws |archive-date=20 September 2024|url-status=live}}</ref> On 27 February 2025, the government introduced the [[Term of Parliament (Enabling 4-year Term) Legislation Amendment Bill]] to conduct a referendum.<ref>{{cite web |author=<!-- not stated --> |date=27 February 2025 |title=Term of Parliament (Enabling 4-year Term) Legislation Amendment Bill |url=https://bills.parliament.nz/v/6/D259D3B7-961B-4D71-C262-08DD56A9BDE4? |website=New Zealand Parliament}}</ref>

===Immigration===
On 3 May 2023, Luxon acknowledged the historical sensitivity around the [[Dawn raids (New Zealand)|dawn raids]] of the 1970s which disproportionately targeted [[Pasifika New Zealanders]]. However, he stated that [[Immigration New Zealand]] needed to "reserve the option" to use [[police raid]]s against individuals involved in serious criminal offending or who posed a security risk to New Zealand. Luxon also stated there were 14,000 overstayers in New Zealand. Luxon's remarks came in response to media coverage of the agency's recent dawn raid tactics against visa overstayers, which had attracted criticism from Deputy Prime Minister [[Carmel Sepuloni]] for re-traumatising the Pasifika community.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Quinlivan |first1=Mark |title=Immigration New Zealand needs to 'reserve the option' to use dawn-raid style tactics, Christopher Luxon says |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2023/05/immigration-new-zealand-needs-to-reserve-the-option-to-use-dawn-raid-style-tactics-christopher-luxon-says.html |access-date=4 May 2023 |work=[[Newshub]] |date=3 May 2023 |archive-date=4 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230504032452/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2023/05/immigration-new-zealand-needs-to-reserve-the-option-to-use-dawn-raid-style-tactics-christopher-luxon-says.html |url-status=dead}}</ref>

On 11 December 2023, Luxon stated that New Zealand's 118,000 annual net migration rate was unsustainable and that infrastructure needed to be managed better to support growth. Luxon made these remarks after the Australian Government announced a new migration strategy to address pressure on housing and infrastructure in Australia.<ref>{{cite news |title=Prime Minister Christopher Luxon: High net migration not sustainable for New Zealand |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/504428/prime-minister-christopher-luxon-high-net-migration-not-sustainable-for-new-zealand |access-date=25 December 2023 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=11 December 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231217021436/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/504428/prime-minister-christopher-luxon-high-net-migration-not-sustainable-for-new-zealand |archive-date=17 December 2023|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Iran===
Following the [[2026 Iran war]] which began on 28 February 2026, Luxon and Foreign Minister [[Winston Peters]] issued a joint statement defending the strikes as a response to Iranian "threats to international peace and security" and called for a resumption of negotiations and adherence to international law.<ref>{{cite press release |last1=Luxon |first1=Christopher |last2=Peters |first2=Winston |title=NZ Government Statement on Iran |url=https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/nz-government-statement-iran |website=Beehive.govt.nz |publisher=[[New Zealand Government]] |access-date=1 March 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260301045403/https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/nz-government-statement-iran |archive-date=1 March 2026 |date=1 March 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 3 March 2026, Luxon said he had misspoke when he said that New Zealand had long supported "any actions" to prevent Iran from obtaining nuclear weapons. He subsequently clarified that he meant that New Zealand supported "action" to prevent Iran from acquiring nuclear weapons.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hu |first1=Justin |title=Luxon says he 'misspoke' on 'any actions' in Iran |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/03/03/luxon-says-he-misspoke-on-any-actions-in-iran/ |work=[[1News]] |access-date=5 March 2026 |date=3 March 2026}}</ref> Luxon later stated in Parliament that the New Zealand Government would take a "pragmatic approach" to visa holders whose travel plans has been disrupted by the Iran war in response to a question by Greens co-leader [[Chlöe Swarbrick]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ensor |first1=Jamie |title=Christopher Luxon returns to Parliament to 'correct answer' on visas during Middle East conflict, but Greens say he mucked up again|url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/christopher-luxon-returns-to-parliament-to-correct-answer-on-visas-during-middle-east-conflict-but-greens-say-he-mucked-up-again/4H2AV5TKDND2ZH43IAEN4S4MS4/ |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |access-date=5 March 2026 |date=3 March 2026}}</ref>

===Māori issues===
In late January 2023, Luxon stated that National opposed [[co-governance]] in the delivery of public services such as health, education and critical infrastructure. Nevertheless, he expressed support for "self-driven" initiatives within the Māori community such as [[Whānau Ora]], [[kohanga reo]] and [[Charter schools in New Zealand|charter schools]].<ref>{{cite news |title=National at Rātana: Luxon confronts co-governance |url=https://www.odt.co.nz/news/national/national-r%C4%81tana-luxon-confronts-co-governance |access-date=7 February 2023 |work=[[Otago Daily Times]] |date=24 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230205022324/https://www.odt.co.nz/news/national/national-r%C4%81tana-luxon-confronts-co-governance |archive-date=5 February 2023|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Wade |first1=Amelia |title=Rātana gets political: Christopher Luxon calls co-governance conversation 'divisive, immature' |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2023/01/r-tana-gets-political-christopher-luxon-calls-co-governance-conversation-divisive-immature.html |access-date=7 February 2023 |work=[[Newshub]] |date=24 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230124055701/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2023/01/r-tana-gets-political-christopher-luxon-calls-co-governance-conversation-divisive-immature.html |archive-date=24 January 2023|url-status=dead}}</ref> On 25 January, Luxon stated that the existence of [[Māori seats]] "doesn't make a lot of sense."<ref>{{cite news |title=Māori seats don't 'make a lot of sense' – Christopher Luxon |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/483083/maori-seats-don-t-make-a-lot-of-sense-christopher-luxon |access-date=7 February 2023 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=25 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230206072206/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/483083/maori-seats-don-t-make-a-lot-of-sense-christopher-luxon |archive-date=6 February 2023|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Christopher Luxon says National will stand candidates in Maori Electorate seats |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/03/christopher-luxon-says-national-will-stand-candidates-on-maori-electorate-seats.html |access-date=7 February 2023 |work=[[Newshub]] |date=5 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220813150800/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/03/christopher-luxon-says-national-will-stand-candidates-on-maori-electorate-seats.html |archive-date=13 August 2022|url-status=dead}}</ref>

During [[Waitangi Day]] on 6 February 2023, Luxon described the [[Treaty of Waitangi]] as a "challenging, imperfect but ultimately inspiring document through which New Zealand had sought to understand what was intended by those who signed it." While acknowledging that the [[New Zealand Crown]] had not upheld the Treaty's promises and obligations, he expressed hope that the [[Treaty of Waitangi claims and settlements|Treaty settlement process]] would be completed by 2030.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Fisher |first1=David |title=Waitangi Day 2023: Politicians wrestle with the rules of not talking politics – then mostly do so anyway |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/politicians-at-waitangi-wrestle-with-the-rules-of-not-talking-politics-then-mostly-do-so-anyway/X756LUI3F5HOPPJSIX7RV76KS4/ |access-date=7 February 2023 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=6 February 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230206035053/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/politicians-at-waitangi-wrestle-with-the-rules-of-not-talking-politics-then-mostly-do-so-anyway/X756LUI3F5HOPPJSIX7RV76KS4/ |archive-date=6 February 2023}}</ref>

During a Question Time in Parliament on 20 August 2024, Luxon said that he believed that Māori ceded sovereignty to the [[New Zealand Crown]] by signing the Treaty of Waitangi. When questioned by [[Green Party of Aotearoa New Zealand|Green]] co-leader [[Chlöe Swarbrick]], Luxon reiterated that the Treaty was New Zealand's founding document and said that it had protected both Māori and Crown interests.

On 19 December 2024, Luxon confirmed that he would not be attending events at the [[Waitangi Treaty Grounds]] on 4 February 2025, stating that it was his intention to celebrate Waitangi Day around New Zealand with different iwi. National Waitangi Trust chairperson Pita Tipene expressed disappointment with Luxon's decision while Labour leader [[Chris Hipkins]] said that Luxon was "running away from problems of his own creation."<ref>{{cite news |title=Prime Minister confirms he won't be at Treaty Grounds on Waitangi Day |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/12/19/prime-minister-confirms-he-wont-be-at-treaty-grounds-on-waitangi-day/ |access-date=27 December 2024 |work=[[1News]] |date=19 December 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241220061600/https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/12/19/prime-minister-confirms-he-wont-be-at-treaty-grounds-on-waitangi-day/ |archive-date=20 December 2024}}</ref>

===Monarchy===
Luxon has described himself as a "soft republican" and believes that New Zealand will "ultimately" become a [[Republicanism in New Zealand|republic]], but that the change would not happen "in my Government and in my time."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/nationals-christopher-luxon-says-no-republic-for-new-zealand-on-his-watch/JIDK5BQURD2HTX5LO6QOX7QCGU/|title=National's Christopher Luxon says no republic for New Zealand on his watch|author=Audrey Young|date=27 September 2022|access-date=26 November 2023|archive-date=7 September 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230907173706/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/nationals-christopher-luxon-says-no-republic-for-new-zealand-on-his-watch/JIDK5BQURD2HTX5LO6QOX7QCGU/|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== School meals ===
On 1 March 2025, Luxon said that the [[School lunches in New Zealand|school lunch programme]] was experiencing "teething issues" in response to quality and delivery issues with the Government's new school lunch programme. He added that Associate Education Minister Seymour "will work his way through those issues... and I expect he will [find a solution]."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Walls |first1=Jason |title=School lunches: Christopher Luxon makes expectations clear to David Seymour as minister responsible |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/school-lunches-christopher-luxon-makes-expectations-clear-to-david-seymour-as-minister-responsible/SUOGHXJ5PFECVEPY2YKASF53HE/ |access-date=3 March 2025 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=1 March 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250303083211/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/school-lunches-christopher-luxon-makes-expectations-clear-to-david-seymour-as-minister-responsible/SUOGHXJ5PFECVEPY2YKASF53HE/ |archive-date=3 March 2025}}</ref> On 4 March, Luxon stated during a press conference that parents who were dissatisfied with the school lunch programme should "make a marmite sandwich and put an apple in a bag".<ref>{{cite news |title=Christopher Luxon on school lunches: 'Go make a Marmite sandwich' |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/543671/christopher-luxon-on-school-lunches-go-make-a-marmite-sandwich |access-date=6 March 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=4 March 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250304005326/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/543671/christopher-luxon-on-school-lunches-go-make-a-marmite-sandwich |archive-date=4 March 2025}}</ref> Luxon's remarks drew criticism from [[Hora Hora School]] principal and Tai Tokerau Principals' Association spokesman Pat Newman, [[Child Poverty Action Group]] executive officer Sarita Divis, [[East Otago High School]] principal Helen Newcombe, who argued that school lunches were needed to improve the health and educational outcomes of students and combat child poverty.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Tswanya |first1=Yolisa |title=Luxon's Marmite comment sparks Northland backlash over school lunch quality |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/northern-advocate/news/luxons-marmite-comment-sparks-northland-backlash-over-school-lunch-quality/ABAEVUUFPBATFDQ5ZSL5Y6XWIM/ |access-date=7 March 2025 |work=Northern Advocate |publisher=[[NZME]] |date=4 March 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250304054659/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/northern-advocate/news/luxons-marmite-comment-sparks-northland-backlash-over-school-lunch-quality/ABAEVUUFPBATFDQ5ZSL5Y6XWIM/ |archive-date=4 March 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=John |first1=Mark |title=PM cops flak over Marmite comment |url=https://www.odt.co.nz/news/dunedin/pm-cops-flak-over-marmite-comment |access-date=7 March 2025 |work=[[Otago Daily Times]] |date=5 March 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250304205807/https://www.odt.co.nz/news/dunedin/pm-cops-flak-over-marmite-comment |archive-date=4 March 2025}}</ref> In response to criticism, Luxon defended his remarks and reiterated the Government's commitment to addressing problems with the school lunch programme.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Jenkins |first1=Ella |title=Luxon says Otago principal 'out of touch' over lunches |url=https://www.odt.co.nz/news/dunedin/luxon-says-otago-principal-out-touch-over-lunches |access-date=7 March 2025 |work=[[Otago Daily Times]] |date=7 March 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250306181927/https://www.odt.co.nz/news/dunedin/luxon-says-otago-principal-out-touch-over-lunches |archive-date=6 March 2025}}</ref>

=== Sex education ===
While Luxon regards sex education as important to the school curriculum, in February 2024 he stated that it should be age appropriate and that parents should be able to withdraw their children from sex education classes if they choose.<ref>{{cite news |title=Christopher Luxon leaves Big Gay Out after heated protest |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/509497/christopher-luxon-leaves-big-gay-out-after-heated-protest |access-date=21 February 2024 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=18 February 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240218091002/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/509497/christopher-luxon-leaves-big-gay-out-after-heated-protest |archive-date=18 February 2024}}</ref>

=== LGBTQ issues ===
In late November 2021, Luxon said the practice of [[conversion therapy]] was "abhorrent".<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/new-national-leader-chris-luxon-on-housing-deal-he-puapua-and-conversion-therapy/4NGXL2TWBG2N3IUV7POF43BDZE/ |title=New National leader: Chris Luxon on housing deal, He Puapua and conversion therapy |date=30 November 2021 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |first=Thomas |last=Coughlan |url-access=subscription |access-date=30 November 2021 |archive-date=30 November 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211130103531/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/new-national-leader-chris-luxon-on-housing-deal-he-puapua-and-conversion-therapy/4NGXL2TWBG2N3IUV7POF43BDZE/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=30 November 2021|title=National's new leader Christopher Luxon talks to Checkpoint|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/national/programmes/checkpoint/audio/2018822690/national-s-new-leader-christopher-luxon-talks-to-checkpoint|access-date=30 November 2021|work=[[Radio New Zealand]]|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211130071640/https://www.rnz.co.nz/national/programmes/checkpoint/audio/2018822690/national-s-new-leader-christopher-luxon-talks-to-checkpoint |archive-date=30 November 2021 }}</ref> In early February 2022, he said he supports [[LGBTQ people in New Zealand|New Zealand's LGBTQ community]] and announced that National MPs would be allowed a conscience vote on the [[Conversion Practices Prohibition Legislation Bill]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Manch |first1=Thomas |title=National Party will allow conscience vote on conversion therapy ban, after block voting against it under Judith Collins |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/127667189/national-party-will-allow-conscience-vote-on-conversion-therapy-ban-after-block-voting-against-it-under-judith-collins |access-date=3 February 2022 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=3 February 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220203114147/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/127667189/national-party-will-allow-conscience-vote-on-conversion-therapy-ban-after-block-voting-against-it-under-judith-collins |archive-date=3 February 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=National Party to allow conscience vote on conversion therapy ban bill |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/460761/national-party-to-allow-conscience-vote-on-conversion-therapy-ban-bill |access-date=3 February 2022 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=2 February 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220203120458/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/460761/national-party-to-allow-conscience-vote-on-conversion-therapy-ban-bill |archive-date=3 February 2022}}</ref> The Bill passed its third and final reading on 15 February 2022,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Blades |first1=Johnny |title=Abortion Safe Areas – a rights balancing act |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/national/programmes/the-house/audio/2018830793/abortion-safe-areas-a-rights-balancing-act |access-date=16 February 2022 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=16 February 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220216073735/https://www.rnz.co.nz/national/programmes/the-house/audio/2018830793/abortion-safe-areas-a-rights-balancing-act|archive-date=16 February 2022|url-status=live}}</ref> with Luxon voting in favour of the Bill's passage.<ref>{{cite web |title=Conversion Practices Prohibition Legislation Bill — Third Reading |url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/pb/hansard-debates/rhr/combined/HansDeb_20220215_20220215_28 |publisher=[[New Zealand Parliament]] |access-date=17 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220614220807/https://www.parliament.nz/en/pb/hansard-debates/rhr/combined/HansDeb_20220215_20220215_28 |archive-date=14 June 2022 |date=15 February 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref>

When asked in March 2023 if anti-transgender activist [[Kellie-Jay Keen-Minshull|Posie Parker]] should be allowed to enter New Zealand, Luxon said that he affirmed people's right to [[free speech]] but that he "absolutely" supported the [[Transgender rights in New Zealand|rights of New Zealand's transgender community]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ensor |first1=Jamie |title=National's Christopher Luxon says he supports trans community, Kiwis expressing identity 'without persecution', but not opposing Posie Parker's NZ travel |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2023/03/national-s-christopher-luxon-says-he-supports-trans-community-kiwis-expressing-identity-without-persecution-but-not-opposing-posie-parker-s-nz-travel.html |access-date=17 October 2023 |work=[[Newshub]] |date=24 March 2023 |archive-date=10 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230510022003/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2023/03/national-s-christopher-luxon-says-he-supports-trans-community-kiwis-expressing-identity-without-persecution-but-not-opposing-posie-parker-s-nz-travel.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> In mid-August, Luxon said that he believes there is no need for [[Bathroom bill|laws specifying which toilets transgender people can use]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Desmarais |first=Felix |date=17 August 2023 |title=Luxon says NZ First transgender bathrooms policy 'on another planet' |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2023/08/17/luxon-says-nz-first-transgender-bathrooms-policy-on-another-planet/ |access-date=14 October 2023 |work=[[1News]] |language=en |archive-date=19 August 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230819071257/https://www.1news.co.nz/2023/08/17/luxon-says-nz-first-transgender-bathrooms-policy-on-another-planet/ |url-status=live }}</ref> During a [[TVNZ]] debate with Prime Minister Chris Hipkins in September, he said that the participation of [[transgender people in sports]] is an issue that should be left to sporting bodies.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Swift |first1=Molly |title=Changing name to Aotearoa, transgender sport and speeding fines: Where Hipkins, Luxon stand on popular issues |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2023/09/changing-name-to-aotearoa-transgender-sport-and-speeding-fines-where-hipkins-luxon-stand-on-popular-issues.html |access-date=19 October 2023 |work=[[Newshub]] |date=19 September 2023 |archive-date=15 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231015041031/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2023/09/changing-name-to-aotearoa-transgender-sport-and-speeding-fines-where-hipkins-luxon-stand-on-popular-issues.html |url-status=dead }}</ref>

==Personal life==

Luxon met his wife Amanda at a church youth group<ref name="Bradley-2013"/> and they married on 8 January 1994<ref>{{cite news |last1=Neville |first1=Sophie |title=Christopher and Amanda Luxon share their family Christmas traditions |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/lifestyle/christopher-and-amanda-luxon-share-their-family-christmas-traditions/QOSPGJT22ZBR3GLEMTWKLA2PBY/ |access-date=9 January 2024 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=16 December 2023 |archive-date=9 January 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240109043409/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/lifestyle/christopher-and-amanda-luxon-share-their-family-christmas-traditions/QOSPGJT22ZBR3GLEMTWKLA2PBY/ |url-status=live }}</ref> when he was 23.<ref name="Trevett-2020"/> They have a son and daughter. The couple share the same faith, saying "it quietly guides what they care about".<ref name="Pellegrino">{{cite news |last1=Pellegrino |first1=Nicky |title=What really matters to Amanda and Christopher Luxon |url=https://www.nowtolove.co.nz/lifestyle/sex-relationships/christopher-luxon-wife-amanda-relationship-47342 |access-date=2 February 2024 |work=Now To Love |language=en |archive-date=2 February 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240202214642/https://www.nowtolove.co.nz/lifestyle/sex-relationships/christopher-luxon-wife-amanda-relationship-47342 |url-status=live }}</ref>

In late July 2022, Luxon confirmed he was on a family holiday in Hawaii during the parliamentary recess when a Facebook video post published on 21 July implied he was visiting [[Te Puke]] at that time. Luxon attributed the confusion to a delay in his social media team updating his whereabouts over the previous week, which he said was a mistake.<ref>{{cite news |title=Luxon post suggested he was in Te Puke – he was actually in Hawaii |url=https://www.odt.co.nz/star-news/star-national/luxon-post-suggested-he-was-te-puke-he-was-actually-hawaii |access-date=6 August 2022 |work=[[Otago Daily Times]] |date=26 July 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220727205641/https://www.odt.co.nz/star-news/star-national/luxon-post-suggested-he-was-te-puke-he-was-actually-hawaii |archive-date=27 July 2022|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Luxon defends holiday in Hawaii despite Te Puke social media post |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2022/07/26/luxon-defends-holiday-in-hawaii-despite-te-puke-social-media-post/ |access-date=6 August 2022 |work=[[1News]] |date=26 July 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220803030750/https://www.1news.co.nz/2022/07/26/luxon-defends-holiday-in-hawaii-despite-te-puke-social-media-post/ |archive-date=3 August 2022|url-status=live}}</ref>

Luxon had a Catholic upbringing,<ref name="Trevett-2020" /> but describes himself as a non-[[Religious denomination|denominational]] Christian.<ref>{{Cite web|title='No religion should dictate to the state' — National's Chris Luxon defends his Christianity in maiden speech|url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2021/03/24/no-religion-should-dictate-to-the-state-nationals-chris-luxon-defends-his-christianity-in-maiden-speech/|access-date=8 December 2021|work=[[1News]]|archive-date=29 November 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211129055906/https://www.1news.co.nz/2021/03/24/no-religion-should-dictate-to-the-state-nationals-chris-luxon-defends-his-christianity-in-maiden-speech/|url-status=live}}</ref> After he returned to New Zealand in 2011 he attended the Upper Room church in Auckland. He now does not go to a regular church, adding that he does not even go to church every Sunday.<ref name="Trevett-2020">{{cite news |title=National Party's Chris Luxon on God, John Key, ambition and sniffing capsicums |first=Claire |last=Trevett |date=8 February 2020 |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/national-partys-chris-luxon-on-god-john-key-ambition-and-sniffing-capsicums/ETXRUJAZ2WU53CNRZFEHXRUHW4/ |newspaper=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |access-date=27 November 2021 |archive-date=27 November 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211127012208/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/national-partys-chris-luxon-on-god-john-key-ambition-and-sniffing-capsicums/ETXRUJAZ2WU53CNRZFEHXRUHW4/ |url-status=live }}</ref>  In his maiden speech in Parliament, he said "My faith is personal to me. It is not in itself a political agenda."<ref>[https://www.stuff.co.nz/business/industries/127097008/former-air-nz-ceo-christopher-luxon-showed-enormous-intellectual-capability-but-rarely-met-with-ordinary-workers Former Air NZ CEO Christopher Luxon showed 'enormous' intellectual capability but rarely met with 'ordinary workers'], Stuff, 30 November 2021.</ref>

Luxon has said that he has [[teetotalism|never drunk alcohol]]. He made that decision at the age of about seven or eight and has cited his grandfather's drinking problems as his reason.<ref>{{Cite news |date=29 May 2025 |title=Luxon says grandfather's drinking behind decision to go teetotal |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2025/05/29/luxon-says-grandfathers-drinking-behind-decision-to-go-teetotal/ |access-date=16 December 2025 |work=[[1News]]}}</ref>

Luxon enjoys [[DIY]], listening to [[country music]], [[waterski]]ing and fishing.<ref>{{Cite news|date=30 November 2021 |title=Watch: National Party's new leader Chris Luxon and deputy Nicola Willis speak after vote |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/456897/watch-national-party-s-new-leader-chris-luxon-and-deputy-nicola-willis-speak-after-vote |access-date=7 August 2022 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |language=en-nz |archive-date=7 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220807230738/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/456897/watch-national-party-s-new-leader-chris-luxon-and-deputy-nicola-willis-speak-after-vote |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |first=Jamie |last=Ensor |title=Christopher Luxon offers to wear cowboy hat during interview, help The AM Show hosts water-ski |language=en |work=[[Newshub]] |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2021/12/christopher-luxon-offers-to-wear-cowboy-hat-during-interview-help-the-am-show-hosts-water-ski.html |access-date=7 August 2022 |archive-date=7 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220807230137/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2021/12/christopher-luxon-offers-to-wear-cowboy-hat-during-interview-help-the-am-show-hosts-water-ski.html |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref name="Pellegrino"/> Luxon is a supporter of the [[Crusaders (rugby union)|Crusaders]] [[rugby union]] team<ref>{{cite web |title=Prime Minister Christopher Luxon's advice for the Crusaders |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/04/22/prime-minister-christopher-luxons-advice-for-the-crusaders/ |work=[[1News]] |access-date=26 April 2024 |archive-date=26 April 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240426070237/https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/04/22/prime-minister-christopher-luxons-advice-for-the-crusaders/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Williamson |first1=Nathan |title=Write them off at your peril: Kellaway refusing to buy into downfall of Crusaders |url=https://www.rugby.com.au/news/write-them-off-at-your-peril-kellaway-refusing-to-buy-into-downfall-of-crusaders-2024423 |website=Rugby.com.au |date=23 April 2024 |publisher=Australian Rugby Union |access-date=26 April 2024 |archive-date=26 April 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240426070235/https://www.rugby.com.au/news/write-them-off-at-your-peril-kellaway-refusing-to-buy-into-downfall-of-crusaders-2024423 |url-status=live }}</ref> and [[Auckland FC]] in [[association football]].<ref>{{cite web |author1=A-Leagues |title=New Zealand's Prime Minister has picked his A-Leagues team! 👀🇳🇿 It was great to see the Rt. Hon. Christopher Luxon in attendance at the NZ Derby 👏 |url=https://www.instagram.com/reel/DGZ1Z-mpLiL/?igsh=MWwxMGpnM2d0N2Znbg== |website=Instagram |access-date=27 April 2025 |date=23 February 2025}}</ref> He attended Auckland FC's first ever match in the [[A-League Men]] in 2024, where he was pranked by an Auckland FC fan who flicked his nose while posing for a photo.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Chapman |first1=Madeleine |title=Is it all good to flick the prime minister's nose? An argument with myself |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/21-10-2024/is-it-all-good-to-flick-the-prime-ministers-nose-an-argument-with-myself |website=[[The Spinoff]] |access-date=27 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250405175005/https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/21-10-2024/is-it-all-good-to-flick-the-prime-ministers-nose-an-argument-with-myself |archive-date=5 April 2025 |date=21 October 2024 |url-status=live}}</ref>

Luxon has a long-standing friendship with former Prime Minister John Key and used to ask Key questions about the pros and cons of being a prime minister.<ref>[https://www.thepost.co.nz/politics/350081337/sir-john-keys-advice-man-who-would-be-prime-minister#:~:text=So%20yes%20%E2%80%93%20Luxon%20and%20Key,%E2%80%9CI%20have%20no%20idea.%E2%80%9D&text=On%20election%20night%20Key%20doesn,Ultimately%20you%20move%20on.%E2%80%9D Sir John Key's advice for the man who would be prime minister], The Post, 30 September 2023</ref> In 2017, while Luxon was chief executive at Air New Zealand, John Key was appointed as an Air New Zealand director. They both resigned from Air New Zealand in 2019.<ref>[https://www.stuff.co.nz/business/industries/127097008/former-air-nz-ceo-christopher-luxon-showed-enormous-intellectual-capability-but-rarely-met-with-ordinary-workers Former Air NZ CEO Christopher Luxon showed 'enormous' intellectual capability but rarely met with 'ordinary workers'], Stuff 30 November 2021</ref>

==Awards==
In 2015, Luxon was named the Deloitte Top 200 CEO of the Year for his leadership at Air New Zealand.<ref>{{cite news |title=Deloitte Top 200: CEO of the Year – Christopher Luxon, Air NZ |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/business-reports/deloitte-top-200-ceo-of-the-year-christopher-luxon-air-nz/W2R3BPMZQ3SO3SIOKURZHC7JPE/ |access-date=9 May 2025 |agency=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=27 November 2015 |language=en-nz}}</ref>

In 2019, Luxon won a BLAKE Award for his sustainability initiatives at Air New Zealand.<ref>{{cite web |title=Christopher Luxon – BLAKE LEADER 2019 |url=https://www.blakenz.org/person/christopher-luxon/ |access-date=9 May 2025 |website=BLAKE NZ}}</ref>

